WEBVTT

00:00:15.110 --> 00:00:37.890
<v Ira Glass>So two years ago, we did an episode of our show from Penn State University. And one of our producers, Sarah Koenig, was in Paternoville. Paternoville is the tent village of students that springs up before every home football game there, not far from the statue of Joe Paterno, the coach, which is outside of Beaver Stadium. The students sleep out in the cold for a couple nights until the game so they get the best seats. This was right before they played Iowa State.
<v Ira Glass>两年前，我们曾在宾夕法尼亚州立大学制作过一期节目。我们的制片人莎拉·柯尼格（Sarah Koenig）当时就在帕特诺村（Paternoville）。帕特诺村是学生在每场主场橄榄球赛前自发组成的帐篷村，位置离比弗体育场（Beaver Stadium）外乔·帕特诺（Joe Paterno，该校教练）的雕像不远。为了能在比赛中抢到好座位，学生们会在寒风中露宿几晚直到比赛开始。这正是在他们对阵爱荷华州立大学之前。

00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:57.500
<v Male Student 1>Ladies and gentlemen in Paternoville! In 24 hours from now, we will already be up 40 points on Iowa! Hey [UNINTELLIGIBLE], what time is it? 10:43!
<v Male Student 1>帕特诺村的女士们、先生们！从现在起24小时后，我们将领先爱荷华队40分！嘿[听不清]，现在几点了？10点43分！

00:00:57.500 --> 00:01:03.410
<v Students>And Michigan still sucks! Michigan-- [CROWD SINGS]
<v Students>密歇根大学依然烂透了！密歇根——[人群歌唱]

00:01:03.410 --> 00:01:11.910
<v Ira Glass>This is just how much Penn State hates Michigan. They're not even playing Michigan the next day, and they still need to express how much they suck.
<v Ira Glass>这就是宾夕法尼亚州立大学有多讨厌密歇根大学的程度。第二天他们甚至不是和密歇根大学比赛，但他们仍需要表达对对方的厌恶。

00:01:11.910 --> 00:01:20.260
<v Ira Glass>So after hanging out for a while with students who are telling her what football means to them, what Penn State means to them-- in both cases, a lot-- somebody asked Sarah--
<v Ira Glass>在和那些向她诉说橄榄球对他们意味着什么、宾夕法尼亚州立大学对他们意味着什么——无论哪种情况，意义都很重大——的学生们相处一段时间后，有人问莎拉——

00:01:20.260 --> 00:01:28.190
<v Male Student 2>All right, so hang on, you've been firing questions. How are you feeling right Now you're sitting here around a bunch of drunk college kids.
<v Male Student 2>好了，等一下，你一直在提问。你现在感觉如何？你正坐在一群喝醉的大学生中间。

00:01:28.190 --> 00:01:30.560
<v Female Student 1>You're like overwhelmed and surprised at our answers, because you--
<v Female Student 1>你似乎被我们的回答弄得不知所措和惊讶，因为你……

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:44.970
<v Sarah Koenig>I'm very surprised at how you genuinely enthusiastic you are about this school. I've never felt that way about any school-- what's surprising is that you guys aren't, like, cynical about the university at all. Like you're just into it.
<v Sarah Koenig>我对你们对这所学校发自内心的热情感到非常惊讶。我从未对任何学校有过这种感觉——令人惊讶的是，你们对这所大学一点也不愤世嫉俗。你们就是完全沉浸其中。

00:01:44.970 --> 00:01:51.720
<v Female Student 2>There's nothing to be cynical about. They deal with every situation perfectly.
<v Female Student 2>这里没什么好愤世嫉俗的。他们处理每一种情况都堪称完美。

00:01:51.720 --> 00:02:03.980
<v Ira Glass>They deal with every situation perfectly. That's how it looked in 2009. This girl's friends immediately tease her-- well, maybe not every situation. But basically, they agree.
<v Ira Glass>他们处理每一种情况都堪称完美。在2009年看起来确实如此。这个女孩的朋友立即取笑她——好吧，也许不是每种情况。但基本上，他们表示赞同。

00:02:03.980 --> 00:02:21.420
<v Ira Glass>And if you know much about Penn State, you know why it is they feel that way. Coach Joe Paterno's grand experiment was to create a football program that would not only win games, the players would be the kind of students who take academics seriously. They'd be held accountable for going to class. "Success with honor" was its motto.
<v Ira Glass>如果你对宾夕法尼亚州立大学了解较多，就会知道他们为何会有这种感觉。乔·帕特诺教练的伟大实验是建立一个不仅能赢球，而且球员都是认真对待学业的学生项目的橄榄球队。他们必须为上课负责。“成功与荣誉”是它的座右铭。

00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:28.610
<v Ira Glass>Back in 2009, when we did that show, I asked some students what made Penn State football special? And the answer was immediate, it was obvious.
<v Ira Glass>早在2009年我们做那期节目时，我问过一些学生是什么让宾夕法尼亚州立大学的橄榄球与众不同？答案是直接且显而易见的。

00:02:28.610 --> 00:02:36.540
<v Male Student 3>Joe Paterno. Joe Paterno is what brings a lot of the-- it's like we pride ourselves on the things that Joe Paterno preaches.
<v Male Student 3>乔·帕特诺。乔·帕特诺带来了很多……我们以乔·帕特诺所倡导的事情为荣。

00:02:36.540 --> 00:02:41.170
<v Male Student 4>Yeah, graduation rates is a big thing JoePa-- we have one of the highest-- I think we have the highest percent--
<v Male Student 4>是的，毕业率是乔·帕特诺（JoePa）非常看重的一点——我们的比例是最高的之一——我想我们有最高的百分比——

00:02:41.170 --> 00:02:44.030
<v Male Student 3>82%--
<v Male Student 3>82%——

00:02:44.030 --> 00:02:45.930
<v Male Student 4>Highest in the Big Ten, definitely.
<v Male Student 4>绝对是“十大联盟”（Big Ten）中最高的。

00:02:45.930 --> 00:02:52.450
<v Ira Glass>They told me fans are supposed to keep a certain code of behavior toward their rivals. Players aren't allowed to gloat when they do well.
<v Ira Glass>他们告诉我，球迷必须对对手保持某种行为准则。球员在表现出色时不允许幸灾乐祸。

00:02:52.450 --> 00:03:13.290
<v Male Student 4>Yeah, I'll never forget-- one of our players flipped into the end zone scoring a touchdown and got a 15-yard excessive celebration for that. Joe Paterno-- I mean, he's an old man, but he got down the sideline in that kid's face right away, saying, let's do sportsmanship. Exactly.
<v Male Student 4>是的，我永远不会忘记——我们的一名球员在达阵得分后翻了个跟头，结果因为过度庆祝被罚了15码。乔·帕特诺——我的意思是，他是个老人，但他立刻冲到边线对着那孩子的脸，说，我们要讲体育精神。没错。

00:03:13.290 --> 00:03:18.200
<v Ira Glass>This is a point of pride for them. Our sports program does things right.
<v Ira Glass>这是他们引以为豪的一点。我们的体育项目做得很好。

00:03:18.200 --> 00:03:49.700
<v Ira Glass>And this, all of this? This is what went to hell in the last two weeks with the charges that former Penn State Assistant Coach Jerry Sandusky sexually abused boys and Penn state officials knew. If you followed the news even a little bit, you've heard the charges from the grand jury's report. Most damning, Assistant Coach Mike McQueary says that back in 2002, he walked in on Sandusky in the locker room shower-- the grand jury report doesn't use the word "raping," but that's plainly what they're talking about-- raping a boy of about ten years old. McQueary then told Coach Paterno what he saw.
<v Ira Glass>而这一切，所有这一切，在过去两周里随着前宾夕法尼亚州立大学助理教练杰瑞·桑达斯基（Jerry Sandusky）性虐待男童以及校方知情的指控而彻底崩塌。如果你哪怕稍微关注了一下新闻，你一定听过大陪审团报告中的指控。最令人震惊的是，助理教练迈克·麦奎里（Mike McQueary）称，早在2002年，他就在更衣室淋浴间撞见了桑达斯基——大陪审团报告中没有使用“强奸”这个词，但很显然他们说的就是这件事——正在强奸一个大约十岁大的男孩。麦奎里随后告诉了帕特诺教练他所看到的一切。

00:03:49.700 --> 00:04:07.220
<v Ira Glass>Paterno did not call the police, but testified that he instead told the head of the athletic program, Tim Curley, and Penn State administrator Gary Schultz. They did not go to the police either. Instead, they banned Sandusky from bringing children into the locker room. That was it.
<v Ira Glass>帕特诺没有报警，而是作证说他把这件事告诉了体育项目主管蒂姆·库利（Tim Curley）和宾夕法尼亚州立大学行政主管加里·舒尔茨（Gary Schultz）。他们也没有报警。相反，他们禁止桑达斯基带孩子进入更衣室。仅此而已。

00:04:07.220 --> 00:04:32.890
<v Ira Glass>Schultz testified that the university's president, Graham Spanier, approved that decision. Spanier, Schultz, and Curley had their own side of the story. They claim that they had not been told that Sandusky was actually having sex with a child. They didn't know that it went that far. The grand jury notes in its report that it did not find Tim Curley and Gary Schultz's testimony to be entirely credible.
<v Ira Glass>舒尔茨作证说，大学校长格雷厄姆·斯帕尼尔（Graham Spanier）批准了该决定。斯帕尼尔、舒尔茨和库利有他们自己的说法。他们声称没有被告知桑达斯基实际上是在与一名儿童发生性关系。他们不知道事情已经严重到那种程度。大陪审团在报告中指出，他们认为蒂姆·库利和加里·舒尔茨的证词并非完全可信。

00:04:32.890 --> 00:04:42.030
<v Ira Glass>At Penn State, all this news has been like a bomb going off. As you probably heard, students rioted. And then everybody regrouped and tried to figure out what to make of it all.
<v Ira Glass>在宾夕法尼亚州立大学，所有这些消息就像炸弹爆炸一样。正如你可能听说的，学生们发生了骚乱。然后每个人重新聚集，试图弄清楚这一切意味着什么。

00:04:42.030 --> 00:05:08.750
<v Ira Glass>Today on our radio program, we're going to spend the hour at Penn State, try to capture what it feels like there right now. And it is pretty extreme. It really is like people's world has been turned upside down. For context, later in the hour, we're going to hear some excerpts from our original 2009 program about Penn State, which people have been Tweeting and emailing about in the last two weeks, because it gives a sense of just how intense the culture of football is at Penn State. You see why everybody's feelings are running so high.
<v Ira Glass>今天在我们的广播节目中，我们将花一小时的时间在宾夕法尼亚州立大学，试图捕捉现在那里的感受。情况非常极端。人们的世界真的被彻底颠覆了。为了提供背景，本小时稍后我们将播放我们在2009年制作的关于宾夕法尼亚州立大学的原始节目片段，过去两周人们一直在推特和邮件中谈论这些内容，因为它让人感受到宾夕法尼亚州立大学的橄榄球文化有多么强烈。你就会明白为什么每个人的情绪都如此高涨。

00:05:08.750 --> 00:05:15.060
<v Ira Glass>From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life, distributed by Public Radio International. I'm Ira Glass. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>来自芝加哥WBEZ电台，这里是《This American Life》，由公共广播国际公司（PRI）发行。我是艾拉·格拉斯（Ira Glass）。请继续收听。

00:05:15.060 --> 00:05:21.920
<v Ira Glass>["WE ARE PENN STATE" CHANT]
<v Ira Glass>[高呼“我们是宾夕法尼亚州立大学”]

00:05:21.920 --> 00:05:25.370
<v Ira Glass>Act One. Say It Ain't So, Joe.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕。说这不是真的，乔。

00:05:25.370 --> 00:05:33.650
<v Ira Glass>So our producer Sarah Koenig lives in State College, in the town that is the home of Penn State. And she says it has been a weird two weeks.
<v Ira Glass>我们的制片人莎拉·柯尼格住在州立学院（State College），那是宾夕法尼亚州立大学的所在地。她说，过去两周非常诡异。

00:05:33.650 --> 00:05:55.070
<v Sarah Koenig>Here's what it's like to live here right now. 100,000 people are having the same conversation at the same time, everywhere they go. I'm overhearing them in line at the coffee shop, at my son's Taekwondo class, in a neighbor's driveway, as people walk down the sidewalk, talking on their cell phones. The weekly newsletter from my kids' school began, "In light of the truly tragic events," et cetera.
<v Sarah Koenig>这就是现在住在这里的感觉。10万人在同一时间、在任何地方都在进行着同样的对话。我在咖啡店排队时、在我儿子的跆拳道课上、在邻居的车道上、当人们在人行道上边走边打手机时，都能听到他们在谈论。我孩子学校的每周通讯开头写道：“鉴于这一真正悲惨的事件”，等等。

00:05:55.070 --> 00:06:10.040
<v Sarah Koenig>And I'm having these conversations myself with everyone I know, and with many people I don't know. Like the guy behind the counter at Kinko's, who told me he didn't want to talk about it, and then we talked about it for 15 minutes. We can't stop. Because what happened here makes no sense.
<v Sarah Koenig>我自己也在和认识的每一个人，以及许多我不认识的人进行这些对话。就像快印店（Kinko's）柜台后的那个人，他告诉我他不想谈论这件事，然后我们谈了15分钟。我们无法停止。因为发生在这里的事情根本说不通。

00:06:10.040 --> 00:06:15.760
<v Nancy Weinreb>I can almost see how it happened. Almost.
<v Nancy Weinreb>我几乎能看出它是怎么发生的。几乎。

00:06:15.760 --> 00:06:32.520
<v Sarah Koenig>This is Nancy Weinreb. She's lived here for more than 30 years, worked in the university library. Her husband is a professor of organic chemistry, and she knows all the higher-ups involved in this thing. Maybe not well, but just socially, or knows people who knows them. The Spaniers, the Curleys, the Schultzes, She knows Mrs. Schultz.
<v Sarah Koenig>这是南希·温雷布（Nancy Weinreb）。她在这里住了30多年，在大学图书馆工作。她丈夫是有机化学教授，她认识所有卷入此事的高层。也许谈不上很熟，只是社交上的认识，或者认识认识他们的人。斯帕尼尔一家、库利一家、舒尔茨一家。她认识舒尔茨太太。

00:06:32.520 --> 00:06:47.830
<v Nancy Weinreb>I really-- it's hard. It's hard to understand, though. It's hard to understand how the leadership-- I mean, very good people and good men could not do what they should have done.
<v Nancy Weinreb>我真的……很难。但这很难理解。很难理解领导层——我的意思是，非常好的人和好男人，怎么会做不到他们本该做的事。

00:06:47.830 --> 00:06:50.510
<v Sarah Koenig>Because you do you think of them as good men.
<v Sarah Koenig>因为你确实把他们视为好人。

00:06:50.510 --> 00:06:52.760
<v Nancy Weinreb>Mmhm. I do.
<v Nancy Weinreb>嗯，是的。我是这样认为的。

00:06:52.760 --> 00:06:58.250
<v Sarah Koenig>And do you still?
<v Sarah Koenig>那你现在还这么认为吗？

00:06:58.250 --> 00:07:11.200
<v Nancy Weinreb>Well, I think they were terribly wrong. But yes, I guess I do. Yeah.
<v Nancy Weinreb>嗯，我认为他们大错特错了。但是的，我想我还是这么认为。是的。

00:07:11.200 --> 00:07:15.120
<v Sarah Koenig>Are you surprised to hear yourself say you still think of them as good men?
<v Sarah Koenig>听到自己说仍然认为他们是好人，你感到惊讶吗？

00:07:15.120 --> 00:07:21.480
<v Nancy Weinreb>Yes. Yes, I am surprised to hear myself think that, yes.
<v Nancy Weinreb>是的。是的，听到自己这么想，我也很惊讶，是的。

00:07:21.480 --> 00:07:45.030
<v Sarah Koenig>I've heard of a few people who compared this thing to 9/11, which seems melodramatic to me, but in one way, I do see the similarity. This feeling of the whole place suddenly seeming unreal, off its axis. Because how could good men turn out to be bad men? And obviously I don't mean Sandusky here. People have no ambivalence about Sandusky. The confusion is all about the guys around Sandusky, who appear to have let the alleged crimes go on.
<v Sarah Koenig>我听过一些人把这件事比作9/11，这对我来说似乎有些矫情，但在某种程度上，我确实看到了相似之处。这种整个地方突然显得不真实、脱离了轨道的感觉。因为好人怎么会变成坏人呢？显然我在这里指的不是桑达斯基。人们对桑达斯基没有矛盾心理。困惑全在于桑达斯基身边的人，他们似乎放任了这些所谓的罪行继续发生。

00:07:45.030 --> 00:08:08.480
<v Sarah Koenig>I met Nancy Weinreb through her son, Michael Weinreb. Michael is a sportswriter. He wrote a book all about football in the 1980s, a large chunk of which is about Penn State, actually. And he's a columnist for this new online magazine called Grantland. He's been writing great columns since the story erupted two weeks ago. At least half a dozen people told me about them. They're circulating like crazy here. Because he's a local, and he's just as disoriented as we are.
<v Sarah Koenig>我是通过南希·温雷布的儿子迈克尔·温雷布（Michael Weinreb）认识她的。迈克尔是一名体育记者。他写了一本关于20世纪80年代橄榄球的书，其中很大一部分其实是关于宾夕法尼亚州立大学的。他是新在线杂志《Grantland》的专栏作家。自从两周前这件事爆发以来，他一直在写很棒的专栏。至少有六个人向我提起过这些文章。它们在这里流传得很疯狂。因为他是本地人，他和我们一样感到迷茫。

00:08:08.480 --> 00:08:27.730
<v Sarah Koenig>In one of the columns, he wrote, "I can't add a lot to what's been written about the facts of the burgeoning scandal at Penn State, except to tell you how strange it feels to type the phrase 'burgeoning scandal at Penn State.' I know that I'm too emotionally attached to the situation to offer any kind of objective take, though I don't think I realized how emotionally attached I was until this occurred."
<v Sarah Koenig>在其中一篇专栏文章中，他写道：“对于宾夕法尼亚州立大学日益严重的丑闻事实，我无法在已有的报道上增加太多内容，除了告诉你，打出‘宾夕法尼亚州立大学日益严重的丑闻’这句话感觉有多么奇怪。我知道我对这种情况情感上投入太多，无法提供任何客观的看法，尽管在事情发生之前，我并没有意识到自己投入得有多深。”

00:08:27.730 --> 00:09:06.890
<v Sarah Koenig>In another column, he tells a story his mom told him last weekend. Quote, "I think it explains as well as anything how jarring this is for a community that has never experienced such a complete and utter shock to its system. My mother's friends-- let us call them the Xes-- are cordial with Graham Spanier, the university president who lost his job a few days before. So last week, Mrs. X baked a coffee cake, got in her car, sweet-talked the security guard stationed in the Spaniers' driveway, and dropped it off at the front door. It was not meant to condone anything Spanier had done. It was merely a way of acknowledging-- well, honestly, I don't know what the hell it was meant to acknowledge, and the Xes didn't, either. But it's just something you do in small towns, isn't it?" Unquote.
<v Sarah Koenig>在另一篇专栏中，他讲了一个他母亲上周末告诉他的故事。引述：“我想这最好地解释了对于一个从未经历过如此彻底和完全的系统性冲击的社区来说，这件事有多令人震惊。我母亲的朋友——我们称他们为X夫妇——与格雷厄姆·斯帕尼尔交情不错，就是那位在几天前丢了工作的大学校长。所以上周，X太太烤了一个咖啡蛋糕，坐进车里，对驻守在斯帕尼尔家车道的保安说了好话，把蛋糕留在了前门。这并不是为了宽恕斯帕尼尔所做的任何事。这仅仅是一种致意的方式——好吧，老实说，我不知道它到底意味着什么，X夫妇自己也不知道。但这只是小城镇里你会做的事情，不是吗？”引述结束。

00:09:06.890 --> 00:09:26.170
<v Sarah Koenig>I understand that. What do you do when a massive child rape scandal hits your town? So I called Michael up. We met last Friday at the giant vigil in front of Old Main, Penn State's admin building. Students organized it to recognize sex abuse victims. We talked about, what else, Penn State football.
<v Sarah Koenig>我理解这一点。当一场大规模的儿童强奸丑闻冲击你的小镇时，你该怎么办？所以我给迈克尔打了电话。上周五我们在老主楼（Old Main，宾夕法尼亚州立大学的行政大楼）前的巨大守夜活动中见了面。学生们组织了这次活动来纪念性虐待受害者。我们谈论了——还能谈什么——宾夕法尼亚州立大学的橄榄球。

00:09:26.170 --> 00:09:43.070
<v Michael Weinreb>I mean, I do watch all the games. Like, we went on our honeymoon in Greece this year, and I found myself following the football game on my cell phone at 10 o'clock at night, feeling like an idiot for doing it. Sitting in a hotel lobby in Athens, and I'm following a football game on my cell phone.
<v Michael Weinreb>我的意思是，我确实看所有的比赛。比如，今年我们去希腊度蜜月，我发现自己在晚上10点用手机查看橄榄球比赛，觉得自己像个白痴。坐在雅典的一家酒店大堂里，我用手机追踪一场橄榄球比赛。

00:09:43.070 --> 00:10:08.800
<v Sarah Koenig>It was freezing. We walked to his friend Ryan's car. Michael lives in New York City now, and he'd come back here to go to Saturday's game, and to just be here, to see what it felt like to be back home. He grew up in town, went to Penn State, wrote for the school paper. And he's quick to issue the following disclaimer. He is fully aware that this whole ordeal isn't about him. It's about little kids who were hideously abused, and how it affected him personally, obviously, that does not matter one way or the other.
<v Sarah Koenig>天气很冷。我们走到他朋友瑞安的车旁。迈克尔现在住在纽约市，他回到这里是为了参加周六的比赛，也只是为了待在这里，看看回到家乡是什么感觉。他在镇上长大，就读于宾夕法尼亚州立大学，为校报写过稿。他很快提出了以下免责声明。他完全意识到这一切并不是关于他。这是关于那些受到可怕虐待的小孩，而这对他个人产生的影响，显然，无论如何那都不重要。

00:10:08.800 --> 00:10:14.650
<v Sarah Koenig>And yet, like so many people from State College, he's been deeply freaked out ever since he heard about this thing.
<v Sarah Koenig>然而，就像州立学院的许多人一样，自从听说这件事以来，他一直深受震动。

00:10:14.650 --> 00:11:05.820
<v Michael Weinreb>It doesn't make any sense. It just doesn't compute. So that's a lot of what I was talking about my parents, too, is just how that part of it-- it just doesn't-- it just goes against everything that you thought you understood, you know? I just-- I don't know. I keep waking up every morning and thinking that it's a dream. It still doesn't seem real. I was expecting to come up here and it to feel different in some way, but it's still the same place, you know? Like I don't even know why I'm here, or what I'm supposed to be writing, or what I'm supposed to be doing at this point.
<v Michael Weinreb>这根本说不通。这根本计算不出结果。所以，我和父母谈论的很多内容也都是关于这一部分的——这根本……这违背了你所认为自己理解的一切，你知道吗？我只是……我不知道。我每天早上醒来都以为这是一场梦。它仍然显得不真实。我原本期待来到这里时会有某种不同的感觉，但这依然是同一个地方，你知道吗？就像我甚至不知道我为什么在这里，或者我应该写什么，或者此时此刻我应该做些什么。

00:11:05.820 --> 00:11:17.280
<v Sarah Koenig>We drove over to the football stadium to see if kids were camping out before the game at Paternoville. There was almost no one there. It was too cold. Just two tents were still up, and one had blown over backwards, like a helpless beetle.
<v Sarah Koenig>我们开车去橄榄球场，看看孩子们是否在比赛前露宿在帕特诺村。那里几乎没有人。太冷了。只有两个帐篷还立着，其中一个像一只无助的甲虫一样向后吹倒了。

00:11:17.280 --> 00:11:42.440
<v Sarah Koenig>The three of us, Michael, Ryan, and I, ended up talking until 1:30 in the morning. Like I said earlier, you just can't stop. For my part, I'm basically rubbernecking, upset and appalled by the crash. Michael, though, it's like he's in the pile-up. His memory is tangled up with this mess. With Paterno, with football. He's here to figure out if he can untangle it. He's worried his past, his childhood, might be contaminated by all this stuff.
<v Sarah Koenig>我们三个——迈克尔、瑞安和我——最终一直聊到凌晨1点半。正如我之前所说，你就是无法停止。就我而言，我基本上是在看热闹，对这场车祸感到不安和震惊。但迈克尔，他就像是在车祸现场。他的记忆与这团乱麻纠缠在一起。与帕特诺纠缠，与橄榄球纠缠。他在这里是为了弄清楚他是否能理清这一切。他担心他的过去，他的童年，可能会被所有这些事情污染。

00:11:42.440 --> 00:12:22.110
<v Michael Weinreb>And so a lot of the good memories I have, you know, father-son stuff that-- I'm starting to sound like it's Field of Dreams or something all of a sudden. But I don't know how that stuff changes. I don't know if it changes, you know? When I think of, like, Penn State beating Nebraska in 1982, and like my dad lifting me on his shoulders, because I couldn't see anything, because everybody was standing up-- and then the last play of the game-- that was like one of the few times I've seen my dad actually shout and get excited about something.
<v Michael Weinreb>所以，我拥有的许多美好回忆，你知道，父子之间的那些事情……我开始听起来像是《梦幻成真》（Field of Dreams）之类的电影了。但我不知道这些事情如何改变。我不知道它是否会改变，你知道吗？当我想到1982年宾夕法尼亚州立大学击败内布拉斯加大学，就像我父亲把我举在他的肩膀上，因为我什么也看不见，因为每个人都站着——然后是比赛的最后一球——那是我见过父亲真正为某事大喊大叫、感到兴奋的少数几次之一。

00:12:22.110 --> 00:12:35.500
<v Michael Weinreb>But I don't know. I don't know how it will affect all that stuff. Because Joe Paterno is there. He was an integral part of all that stuff.
<v Michael Weinreb>但我不知道。我不知道它会如何影响所有这些事情。因为乔·帕特诺在那里。他是所有这些事情中不可或缺的一部分。

00:12:35.500 --> 00:13:10.640
<v Sarah Koenig>I don't know if you noticed how easily he referenced that Penn State-Nebraska game from 1982. I heard Michael do this over and over. Football is how he marks time, an organizing principle for his life. He remembers watching the Sugar Bowl in 1979 in the family room when he was 6, and then going outside in the snow with his next door neighbor and replaying the game so Penn State would win. "To this day," he wrote one of his recent columns, "When I try to recall the combination of my gym locker, or a friend's birthday, or the license plate of my rental car, I think in terms of uniform numbers. It's not 31-17-03. It's Shane Conlan, Harry Hamilton, Chip La Barca."
<v Sarah Koenig>我不知道你是否注意到他是多么自然地提到了1982年那场宾夕法尼亚州立大学对阵内布拉斯加大学的比赛。我听到迈克尔一遍又一遍地这样做。橄榄球是他标记时间的方式，是他生活的组织原则。他记得6岁时在客厅看1979年的糖碗（Sugar Bowl），然后和隔壁邻居去雪地里重演比赛，这样宾夕法尼亚州立大学就能赢。“直到今天，”他在最近的一篇专栏文章中写道，“当我试图回想我的健身房储物柜密码、朋友的生日或我的租赁汽车车牌时，我都会用球衣号码来思考。不是31-17-03。而是肖恩·康兰（Shane Conlan）、哈里·汉密尔顿（Harry Hamilton）、奇普·拉·巴尔卡（Chip La Barca）。”

00:13:10.640 --> 00:13:32.390
<v Michael Weinreb>Penn State played Miami here, and they got killed. And I think that was the last game before September 11. For some reason, I just marked it-- I remember going to the first game, I think it was the first game after September 11, when they played Wisconsin.
<v Michael Weinreb>宾夕法尼亚州立大学在这里对阵迈阿密大学，结果被彻底击溃了。我想那是9/11事件前的最后一场比赛。出于某种原因，我只是标记了它……我记得去看了第一场比赛，我想是9/11之后对阵威斯康星大学的那场。

00:13:32.390 --> 00:13:43.850
<v Sarah Koenig>I find this a little crazy, and so does he, that he's bookended September 11 with Penn State football games. And he will, no doubt, mark this thing happening now with tomorrow's game against Nebraska.
<v Sarah Koenig>我觉得这有点疯狂，他自己也这么认为，他竟然用宾夕法尼亚州立大学的橄榄球比赛来界定9/11事件的前后。毫无疑问，他也会用明天对阵内布拉斯加大学的比赛来标记现在发生的这件事。

00:13:43.850 --> 00:14:16.330
<v Sarah Koenig>Here's what he wrote in another column. "I do not believe Paterno deserves our sympathy right now, and in fact, I walked around State College this weekend supremely pissed off that he did not live up to the standards he would like us to believe he set for himself. And I was not alone in that sentiment. And yet when I saw those televised shots of his house on McKee Street, something caught in my throat. The conditioned response to a man I've been raised to believe is the moral arbiter of our community."
<v Sarah Koenig>他在另一篇专栏文章中写道：“我不认为帕特诺现在值得我们同情，事实上，这个周末我在州立学院走来走去，对未能达到他希望我们相信他为自己设定的标准感到无比愤怒。而这种情绪并非我一人独有。然而，当我看到电视上播出他在麦基街（McKee Street）的房子的镜头时，我的喉咙里有什么东西哽住了。这是一个我从小被灌输要相信是我们的社区道德仲裁者的人的条件反射。”

00:14:16.330 --> 00:14:32.890
<v Sarah Koenig>And of course, Michael's not alone in this. In State College, confusion has seeped into everyone's house. You can't believe it happened, or that it happened the way they're saying it happened in the grand jury report. There must be something we don't know, some key moment, some key insight, some key fact that will make it add up.
<v Sarah Koenig>当然，迈克尔并不是唯一有这种感觉的人。在州立学院，困惑已经渗入了每个人的家中。你无法相信这件事发生了，或者说你无法相信它竟然是以大陪审团报告中描述的那样发生。一定有什么我们不知道的事情，某个关键时刻，某个关键见解，某个关键事实，能让这一切讲得通。

00:14:32.890 --> 00:15:01.400
<v Sarah Koenig>So thousands and thousands of people here, including me, are turning over theories. A former State College cop told me the other day, the football program is run like the mafia, and Paterno is the godfather. This cop had had suspicions about Sandusky for years, saw him hug a Little League kid a little too enthusiastically one time, and had seen him hanging around high school sports events, and had actually spoken to Sandusky about it, asked him, why exactly was he there? So this cop things Paterno and all the others around the football program must've known for a long time.
<v Sarah Koenig>所以这里成千上万的人，包括我在内，都在琢磨各种理论。一位前州立学院警察前几天告诉我，橄榄球项目就像黑手党一样运作，帕特诺就是教父。这位警察多年来一直对桑达斯基有怀疑，曾有一次看到他太热情地拥抱了一个小联盟的孩子，也曾看到他在高中体育赛事附近转悠，并实际上就此事质问过桑达斯基，问他到底为什么在那里？所以这位警察认为帕特诺和橄榄球项目周围的所有人肯定早就知道了。

00:15:01.400 --> 00:15:19.990
<v Sarah Koenig>Paterno obviously has a lot of defenders, too, or at least people looking very hard for a reason not to condemn him. I met a football fan slash Penn State Law student named Chris who said he and all his friends in the law program are very skeptical of the grand jury report. Grand jury reports are supposed to be one-sided, he said, and there are so many inconsistencies.
<v Sarah Koenig>显然，帕特诺也有很多捍卫者，或者至少是那些非常努力地寻找理由不去谴责他的人。我遇到了一个既是橄榄球迷又是宾夕法尼亚州立大学法学院学生的克里斯，他说他和他在法学院的所有朋友都对大陪审团的报告非常怀疑。他说，大陪审团报告理应是片面的，而且里面有太多的不一致之处。

00:15:19.990 --> 00:15:31.310
<v Chris>It could be complete reversal. We may find out, hypothetically, that Tim Curley and Gary Schultz are telling the truth, and it's in fact Mike McQueary who is lying.
<v Chris>这可能是完全的颠倒。我们可能会发现，假设蒂姆·库利和加里·舒尔茨说的是实话，而实际上迈克·麦奎里在撒谎。

00:15:31.310 --> 00:15:38.630
<v Sarah Koenig>And this woman, Lori, an alum and Paterno devotee who I met at the vigil last Friday-- she can't believe Paterno would have just let this go.
<v Sarah Koenig>还有这位女士，洛丽，一位上周五我在守夜活动中遇到的校友和帕特诺的忠实信徒——她无法相信帕特诺会就这么放任不管。

00:15:38.630 --> 00:15:45.300
<v Lori>Maybe, maybe he was told that things were being followed up on, and he was under the impression that they were, but they weren't.
<v Lori>也许，也许有人告诉他事情正在跟进，他以为事情正在处理中，但实际上并没有。

00:15:45.300 --> 00:16:08.850
<v Sarah Koenig>I've heard people blame the cops for being in collusion with Penn State. I've heard cock-a-mamie theories, like people saying, this is all because the Pennsylvania AG wanted to take down Spanier, who fought publicly with the governor over funding earlier this year. The governor, who used to be the AG. I've heard that Paterno promoted Mike McQueary so he'd keep his mouth shut. A lawyer in town told me he blamed the lawyers, said Penn State's lawyers covered this whole thing up.
<v Sarah Koenig>我听到有人指责警察与宾夕法尼亚州立大学勾结。我听到过各种荒谬的理论，比如有人说，这一切都是因为宾夕法尼亚州总检察长想搞垮斯帕尼尔，因为斯帕尼尔今年早些时候曾公开与州长就资金问题发生争执。那位州长曾经是总检察长。我听说帕特诺提拔迈克·麦奎里是为了让他闭嘴。镇上的一位律师告诉我他责怪律师，说宾夕法尼亚州立大学的律师掩盖了整件事。

00:16:08.850 --> 00:16:15.330
<v Sarah Koenig>My friends Kim and Will moved here from Seattle about a decade ago. Our daughters are friends. Will's a bioengineer at Penn State.
<v Sarah Koenig>我的朋友金（Kim）和威尔（Will）大约十年前从西雅图搬到这里。我们的女儿是朋友。威尔是宾夕法尼亚州立大学的生物工程师。

00:16:15.330 --> 00:16:21.370
<v Will>I'm one that embraces denial as much as the next person.
<v Will>我是那种和任何人一样乐于拥抱否认的人。

00:16:21.370 --> 00:16:35.100
<v Sarah Koenig>Denial is a big theory here, too. Not just on an institutional level, on a personal level. That some combination of Paterno, Curley, Schultz, Spanier simply could not, or would not, believe such grotesque crimes were happening in their locker rooms.
<v Sarah Koenig>否认在这里也是一个重要的理论。不仅仅是在机构层面，在个人层面也是如此。帕特诺、库利、舒尔茨、斯帕尼尔的某种组合根本不能，或不愿相信在他们的更衣室里正在发生如此令人作呕的罪行。

00:16:35.100 --> 00:17:12.300
<v Will>There are so many reasons to tell yourself that you're not exactly sure. So you're telling yourself you have to be so sure, and you're not 100% sure, and so, well, it wasn't exactly illegal, or I don't need to do this, I only need to go this far, or somebody else is going to do it, or maybe he didn't see what he thought he saw, and things like that. There's so much incentive to try to want it to not be true. And then you just kind of wish it away.
<v Will>有太多理由告诉自己你并不完全确定。所以你告诉自己你必须非常确定，但你并没有百分之百确定，所以，嗯，它并不完全违法，或者我不需要做这个，我只需要做到这一步，或者别人会去做，或者也许他没看到他以为自己看到的东西，诸如此类。人们有太大的动力去试图想要它不是真的。然后你就只是想把它驱逐出脑海。

00:17:12.300 --> 00:17:33.600
<v Sarah Koenig>The crux of the riddle for a lot of people is, did Mike McQueary really tell Joe Paterno and other university officials exactly what he's now saying he saw? That is, Jerry Sandusky in the shower, raping a boy who looked to be about ten years old? McQueary says there was no ambiguity about what he told them. They, of course, say that it was never clear to them the shower incident was of a, quote, "sexual nature."
<v Sarah Koenig>对许多人来说，谜题的核心在于，迈克·麦奎里是否真的告诉了乔·帕特诺和其他大学官员他现在所说的他所看到的？也就是说，杰瑞·桑达斯基在淋浴间强奸了一个看起来大约十岁大的男孩？麦奎里说他告诉他们的话没有任何歧义。而他们当然说，淋浴事件属于“性性质”这件事对他们来说从来都不清楚。

00:17:33.600 --> 00:17:56.670
<v Sarah Koenig>So how do you square that? I've heard people say they believe that maybe McQueary didn't quite get the information out. There he is, sitting on the sofa across from Paterno, a football legend, his mentor, a 70-something-year-old man, a Catholic-- and is he really going to paint the picture? The shower, a little boy, ugh. Like one of my friends said, it could be that something got lost in the telling.
<v Sarah Koenig>那么你如何调和这一点呢？我听到有人说他们相信也许麦奎里没有把信息传达清楚。他当时坐在帕特诺对面的沙发上，帕特诺是一个橄榄球传奇，是他的导师，一个70多岁的老人，一个天主教徒——他真的会描绘出那种画面吗？淋浴间，一个小男孩，呃。就像我的一个朋友说的，可能在讲述的过程中漏掉了什么东西。

00:17:56.670 --> 00:18:14.970
<v Sarah Koenig>Kim of Kim and Will is a psychotherapist. She thinks Spanier, the university president, got all turned around in his decision-making, partly because of the closed circle he lived in, where administrators talk mostly to other administrators. Spanier has said he never knew about the sexual assaults. Spanier, by the way, is also a family therapist.
<v Sarah Koenig>金（Kim）是一名心理治疗师。她认为大学校长斯帕尼尔在决策上完全糊涂了，部分原因是他生活在一个封闭的圈子里，行政人员主要只和其他行政人员交流。斯帕尼尔曾说他从来不知道这些性侵事件。顺便说一句，斯帕尼尔也是一名家庭治疗师。

00:18:14.970 --> 00:18:23.450
<v Sarah Koenig>Do you feel like, at the end of the day, he knew or he didn't know the particulars? Spanier?
<v Sarah Koenig>你觉得最终，他是知道还是不知道具体细节？斯帕尼尔？

00:18:23.450 --> 00:18:27.840
<v Kim>I feel like he probably did. Yes, I do.
<v Kim>我觉得他大概是知道的。是的，我确实这么认为。

00:18:27.840 --> 00:18:41.230
<v Sarah Koenig>Mmhm. Does that make him a bad guy? Like, how do you get your head around that?
<v Sarah Koenig>嗯。那这让他成为坏人了吗？你是怎么想通这一点的？

00:18:41.230 --> 00:19:15.920
<v Kim>Maybe from being basically kind of a politician, right, for such a long time? That he might have lost touch with some sort of boots-on-the-ground morals that the rest of us can live with? And he's dealing with large groups of people, and large sums of money, and I could imagine him confusing his duty to the university, and maybe even to the community, with his moral obligation to protect the innocent.
<v Kim>也许是因为他长期以来基本上是个政客，对吧？他可能已经与我们其他人能接受的那种脚踏实地的道德失去了联系？他在处理大群人，处理大笔资金，我可以想象他把对大学，甚至可能是对社区的责任，与保护无辜者的道德义务搞混了。

00:19:15.920 --> 00:19:28.030
<v Sarah Koenig>My friend Eileen grew up about an hour and a half from Penn State, in farming country. Eileen used to live across the street from McQueary's wife and mother-in-law, and she believes McQueary. That he told the higher-ups exactly what he saw, and they buried it.
<v Sarah Koenig>我的朋友艾琳（Eileen）在距离宾夕法尼亚州立大学约一个半小时路程的农耕地区长大。艾琳过去住在麦奎里妻子和岳母的街对面，她相信麦奎里。相信他告诉了上层他所看到的真相，而他们掩盖了这一切。

00:19:28.030 --> 00:19:51.920
<v Eileen>In their world that kind of stuff didn't happen. And they couldn't deal with it. I mean, we were all having trouble dealing with the fact that it happened, and he was like a brother, I guess, in the football family, right? And so how can you deal with a family member doing something like that to kids?
<v Eileen>在他们的世界里，那种事情是不会发生的。他们无法处理它。我的意思是，我们都在为这件事发生了这一事实而苦恼，而他就像橄榄球大家庭里的一个兄弟，对吧？所以你该怎么处理一个家庭成员对孩子做这种事呢？

00:19:51.920 --> 00:20:30.340
<v Eileen>Maybe what they said was, you know, it was one time-- they had a big boy meeting, and they sat him down, they had a sit-down, and they said you're not going to do this again, right? And he, of course, said, oh, yeah, yeah, no, not a thing, not an issue. And they used that to make themselves feel better, and for the greater good of all of Penn State. And I want to think they thought they were doing the right thing. I think JoePa did, right? That's a skewed vision, right? Of--
<v Eileen>也许他们说的是，你知道，那只有一次——他们开了一个大男孩会议，让他坐下来，进行了一次谈话，说你不能再这样做了，对吧？而他当然说，哦，是的，是的，没别的事了，没问题了。他们利用这一点来让自己感觉好受些，并且是为了宾夕法尼亚州立大学的更大利益。我想他们以为自己在做正确的事。我想乔·帕特诺也是这么想的，对吧？那是一种扭曲的愿景，对吧？

00:20:30.340 --> 00:20:33.870
<v Sarah Koenig>Oh, you think they thought they were doing the right thing.
<v Sarah Koenig>哦，你认为他们以为自己在做正确的事。

00:20:33.870 --> 00:20:58.940
<v Eileen>I think, at least-- and I'm not giving them an out here. But yeah, I think in that twisted world, he thought it'll serve more kids, and it'll be for the greater good if we just make this go away so that the football program can keep on doing what it does. And I'm not a-- oh, god. I'm not a football person.
<v Eileen>我认为，至少……我不是在为他们开脱。但是的，我想在那个扭曲的世界里，他认为如果我们让这件事消失，以便橄榄球项目能继续做它所做的事情，这对更多的孩子有好处，是为了更大利益。我不是一个……噢，天哪。我不是一个橄榄球爱好者。

00:20:58.940 --> 00:21:00.630
<v Sarah Koenig>Were you just nervous to say that on tape?
<v Sarah Koenig>你刚才在录音时是不是很紧张？

00:21:00.630 --> 00:21:05.040
<v Eileen>Yes!
<v Eileen>是的！

00:21:05.040 --> 00:21:06.810
<v Sarah Koenig>Explain that. Why?
<v Sarah Koenig>解释一下。为什么？

00:21:06.810 --> 00:21:47.060
<v Eileen>Where do we live, right? I think that's part of this whole thing. Imagine if me, who does not give a [BLEEP] about it, right? Really has some hesitation in this town. And because of the people in my life, some of the people in my life-- yeah, right? And I didn't even realize that, Sarah. That was involuntary. Really. That's ridiculous. It would be like me saying to my sister, who lives in rural Pennsylvania, yeah, I don't really care about the Steelers. I would not be invited for Christmas.
<v Eileen>我们住在哪里，对吧？我想这是整个事情的一部分。想象一下如果像我这样根本不在乎的人，对吧？在这个小镇真的有一些顾虑。而因为我生活中的人，我生活中的一些人……是的，对吧？我甚至没意识到这一点，莎拉。那是无意识的。真的。这很荒谬。这就像我对住在宾夕法尼亚州农村的妹妹说，是的，我真的不在乎钢人队（Steelers）。我绝对不会被邀请去过圣诞节。

00:21:47.060 --> 00:21:56.860
<v Sarah Koenig>On Saturday morning, Michael Weinreb and his parents and some family friends met, like they always do, at the chemistry building where Michael's father works, and then made the trek over to the football game.
<v Sarah Koenig>周六早上，迈克尔·温雷布和他的父母以及一些家庭朋友像往常一样在迈克尔父亲工作的化学楼见面，然后徒步前往橄榄球比赛现场。

00:21:56.860 --> 00:21:58.680
<v Michael Weinreb>Where did the chicken coops used to be?
<v Michael Weinreb>以前鸡舍在哪里？

00:21:58.680 --> 00:22:06.690
<v Steven Weinreb>The chicken coops, we'll pass by where the chicken coops used to be. It's where, there are field hockey fields over there now.
<v Steven Weinreb>鸡舍，我们会经过鸡舍过去所在的地方。现在那里有曲棍球场。

00:22:06.690 --> 00:22:09.500
<v Michael Weinreb>But you'd have to make this walk up there, and you'd have to walk by these awfully--
<v Michael Weinreb>但你得走那条路上去，你得经过这些糟糕的……

00:22:09.500 --> 00:22:11.330
<v Steven Weinreb>Well, it would smell, was part of the problem.
<v Steven Weinreb>嗯，臭味是问题的一部分。

00:22:11.330 --> 00:22:12.460
<v Michael Weinreb>Terrible.
<v Michael Weinreb>糟糕。

00:22:12.460 --> 00:22:21.090
<v Sarah Koenig>This exchange about the chicken coops was the sum total of Michael's communication with his father on the walk over, by the way, which Michael said was typical. His father is not a huge talker.
<v Sarah Koenig>顺便说一句，关于鸡舍的这段对话是迈克尔在走过去的过程中与他父亲沟通的总和，迈克尔说这很典型。他父亲不怎么说话。

00:22:21.090 --> 00:22:29.980
<v Sarah Koenig>We headed up to Beaver Stadium, and again, things were more or less the same as any football Saturday. It was a beautiful day, people everywhere, looking happy.
<v Sarah Koenig>我们前往比弗体育场，同样，情况和任何橄榄球周六大同小异。那是个美好的一天，到处都是人，看起来很高兴。

00:22:29.980 --> 00:22:36.570
<v Michael Weinreb>It just feels so normal right now. And it's odd.
<v Michael Weinreb>现在感觉太正常了。而且很奇怪。

00:22:36.570 --> 00:22:39.480
<v Sarah Koenig>Is that comforting to you, or is it just weird?
<v Sarah Koenig>这对你来说是安慰，还是只是觉得怪怪的？

00:22:39.480 --> 00:22:49.410
<v Michael Weinreb>It's just kind of weird. I feel like I should constantly be apologizing, you know?
<v Michael Weinreb>只是觉得有点怪。我觉得我应该一直道歉，你知道吗？

00:22:49.410 --> 00:22:50.650
<v Sarah Koenig>To whom?
<v Sarah Koenig>向谁？

00:22:50.650 --> 00:23:02.670
<v Michael Weinreb>To everybody. I mean, to the victims, obviously, to people who saw that stuff on TV the other night. It's just like this woman holding up that sign. That's kind of how I feel. You know?
<v Michael Weinreb>向所有人。我的意思是，显然是向受害者，向那天晚上在电视上看到那些东西的人。就像那个举着牌子的女人一样。我差不多就是这种感觉。你知道吗？

00:23:02.670 --> 00:23:03.200
<v Sarah Koenig>What's the sign?
<v Sarah Koenig>牌子上写什么？

00:23:03.200 --> 00:23:07.070
<v Michael Weinreb>It says, "To the victims, I apologize for Penn State."
<v Michael Weinreb>上面写着，“致受害者，我为宾夕法尼亚州立大学道歉。”

00:23:07.070 --> 00:23:15.700
<v Sarah Koenig>Michael shoved a dollar into a box. There was another student collecting money for a child abuse charity, and fans were just shovelling money at them, jamming bills in there.
<v Sarah Koenig>迈克尔往一个盒子里塞了一美元。还有另一个学生在为儿童虐待慈善机构募捐，球迷们只是不停地往里塞钱，把钞票塞进去。

00:23:15.700 --> 00:23:44.130
<v Sarah Koenig>So just to recap. This terrible story breaks open. People in State College are stunned. Then a couple days later, there's a riot-- a big, ugly retching of misplaced anger. And then two days after that, there's a vigil. A massive, solemn gathering that had the double benefit of calming the campus and looking good on the evening news. And then two days after that, there's a football game. And all of a sudden, that off-kilter feeling is gone. People are cheerful, excited. That's how it felt to me, anyway.
<v Sarah Koenig>所以总结一下。这个可怕的故事爆发了。州立学院的人们惊呆了。然后几天后，发生了骚乱——一场巨大的、丑陋的、错位的愤怒呕吐。再过两天，举行了守夜活动。一场大规模、庄严的聚会，既安抚了校园，又在晚间新闻里显得体面。再过两天，有一场橄榄球比赛。突然间，那种失衡的感觉消失了。人们兴高采烈、兴奋不已。无论如何，我就是这种感觉。

00:23:44.130 --> 00:24:03.610
<v Sarah Koenig>And it occurs to me that maybe that's what happened to Paterno and those guys in the administration back when they first got wind of Sandusky's alleged crimes. Just give it some space, squint into the sun, go to a football game, and you can kind of forget about it.
<v Sarah Koenig>我突然想到，也许这就是当初帕特诺和行政部门的那些人在第一次听到桑达斯基所谓的罪行时所发生的情况。只要给它一点空间，眯眼望向太阳，去看一场橄榄球比赛，你就能把这事儿给忘了。

00:24:03.610 --> 00:24:10.670
<v Sarah Koenig>The following morning, on Sunday, Michael told me it did feel different inside the stadium-- more subdued. Even the cheering was subdued.
<v Sarah Koenig>第二天早上，周日，迈克尔告诉我体育场里的感觉确实不一样——更压抑了。即使欢呼声也很压抑。

00:24:10.670 --> 00:24:53.430
<v Michael Weinreb>It was just kind of quiet, even when it was loud. And it's like people were so tired of talking about the situation that they wanted to try to talk about the game, but then you start talking about the game, and you'd be like, how can we talk about the game? And then, you know, there was a moment in the fourth quarter where it was like, Penn State was down 17 to nothing, and they came back and made it 17-14, and then they got a defensive stop and got the ball back, and it was like-- I allowed myself like one moment where I kind of got excited, you know? It was kind of like, OK, I'm going to allow myself to get excited about football for two minutes here. Then they lost, and it was like, I don't care, you know? I didn't care that they lost, you know?
<v Michael Weinreb>即使在声音很大时，也有一种安静的感觉。就像人们太厌倦谈论这件事了，他们想尝试谈论比赛，但当你开始谈论比赛时，你会想，我们怎么能谈论比赛呢？然后，你知道，第四节有一个时刻，宾夕法尼亚州立大学以0比17落后，他们反扑将比分追至14比17，然后他们防守成功拿回了球权，就像……我允许自己有那么一刻感到兴奋，你知道吗？就像是，好的，我允许自己在这里为橄榄球兴奋两分钟。然后他们输了，就像，我不在乎，你知道吗？我不在乎他们输了，你知道吗？

00:24:53.430 --> 00:24:55.710
<v Sarah Koenig>Have you had that feeling before?
<v Sarah Koenig>你以前有过那种感觉吗？

00:24:55.710 --> 00:25:15.540
<v Michael Weinreb>Not quite like this, no. Yeah, see, that's the thing. I think a couple of my friends from college e-mailed me and they were like, I wish we would've won that, you know? And I was like, why? What difference does it make? It didn't matter. It just didn't matter.
<v Michael Weinreb>不像这次这样，没有。是的，看，就是这样。我想我大学的几个朋友发邮件给我，他们说，我真希望我们能赢，你知道吗？而我说，为什么？那有什么区别？那根本不重要。就是不重要。

00:25:15.540 --> 00:25:21.370
<v Sarah Koenig>Did your parents have anything to say about it? Like that it felt different to them, or--
<v Sarah Koenig>你父母有什么要说的吗？比如他们觉得感觉不一样了，或者——

00:25:21.370 --> 00:25:40.350
<v Michael Weinreb>Yeah, some guys in their row who were so drunk that they puked all over themselves. And I was like, that's weirdly like going to a funeral hammered, you know? It's like, just have some respect. First of all, it's a noon game. How do you get that drunk, you know? But--
<v Michael Weinreb>是的，他们那一排有几个家伙醉得呕吐在自己身上。我就想，这奇怪得就像喝得烂醉去参加葬礼，你知道吗？就像，稍微尊重一下。首先，这是一场中午的比赛。你怎么能喝得那么醉，你知道吗？但是——

00:25:40.350 --> 00:25:42.620
<v Sarah Koenig>You know how you get that drunk. You start at 7:30.
<v Sarah Koenig>你知道你怎么喝得那么醉的。你从7点半就开始了。

00:25:42.620 --> 00:25:47.370
<v Michael Weinreb>I know, I know. I've done that before, so I shouldn't say anything.
<v Michael Weinreb>我知道，我知道。我以前也那样做过，所以我没资格说什么。

00:25:47.370 --> 00:26:00.230
<v Sarah Koenig>Honestly, seeing drunk kids stumbling back from the game on Saturday, that was the one thing that made me feel like the town was going back to normal, for better or for worse.
<v Sarah Koenig>说实话，周六看到喝醉的孩子们从比赛现场跌跌撞撞地走回来，那是一件事，让我觉得这个城镇正在恢复正常，不管是好是坏。

00:26:00.230 --> 00:26:18.670
<v Ira Glass>Sarah Koenig. Coming up, we go back in time, through the miracle of audio recording technology, before all this happened, before all the trouble, to the program that we did back in 2009 about Penn State. That's in a minute from Chicago Public Radio and Public Radio International when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>莎拉·柯尼格。接下来，通过音频录制技术的奇迹，我们将回到过去，在那一切发生之前，在所有麻烦之前，回到我们在2009年关于宾夕法尼亚州立大学制作的节目。稍后继续，来自芝加哥公共广播电台和公共广播国际公司，我们的节目继续。

00:26:18.670 --> 00:26:58.260
