00:26:58.260 --> 00:27:33.220
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. This week on our show we've been talking about Penn State and how people there are reacting to grand jury charges that a former assistant coach sexually abused young boys. The charge is that Penn State officials failed to report the abuse to authorities. In this half of the show, we're going to play excerpts from our 2009 episode about Penn State. That show is about mostly the fact that the school was named the nation's number one party school that year, so it's mostly about partying and drinking, which have nothing at all to do with this sex abuse scandal. Just need to get out in front of that right now.
<v Ira Glass>这里是《This American Life》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。本周在我们的节目中，我们一直在讨论宾夕法尼亚州立大学，以及人们对大陪审团指控一名以前的助理教练性虐待年轻男孩的反应。指控称，宾夕法尼亚州立大学的官员未向当局报告这些虐待行为。在本节节目中，我们将播放我们在2009年关于宾夕法尼亚州立大学的那期节目片段。那期节目主要是关于这所学校在那一年被评为全国第一派对学校的事实，所以主要是关于聚会和喝酒，这与这起性虐待丑闻完全无关。我需要立即把这一点说明白。

00:27:33.220 --> 00:27:54.220
<v Ira Glass>But that said, you can't talk about partying and drinking at Penn State without talking about football. Football is a kind of a location where a lot of things like that occur. And though these stories are about drinking, throughout all the stories, there are these moments where you see just how central it is in the life of Penn State. You see the before in the before and after of this scandal.
<v Ira Glass>但话虽如此，如果不谈橄榄球，你就无法谈论宾夕法尼亚州立大学的派对和饮酒。橄榄球是很多类似事情发生的场所。虽然这些故事是关于饮酒的，但在所有故事中，都有一些时刻让你看到它在宾夕法尼亚州立大学的生活中是多么核心。你看到了这场丑闻的前后。

00:27:54.220 --> 00:28:15.290
<v Ira Glass>And in the before-- and this explains a lot about the after-- people love Penn State, and the love was uncomplicated. Students were spontaneously telling all of us how much they loved the school, how they never wanted the college experience to end. They chanted the school chant, "We Are Penn State," not just in the football stadium. It was in the student union, it was in the shuttle bus. They would just chant it, out of the blue.
<v Ira Glass>而在之前——这解释了之后的很多事情——人们热爱宾夕法尼亚州立大学，这种爱是单纯的。学生们自发地告诉我们他们有多热爱这所学校，他们有多么不希望大学生活结束。他们高呼校歌“我们是宾夕法尼亚州立大学”，不仅在橄榄球场。在学生会，在穿梭巴士上，他们都会随口高呼。

00:28:15.290 --> 00:28:19.770
<v Ira Glass>["WE ARE PENN STATE" CHANT]
<v Ira Glass>[高呼“我们是宾夕法尼亚州立大学”]

00:28:19.770 --> 00:28:26.870
<v Ira Glass>They said "We are Penn State" in conversation, to make a point. They were part of this club that they were really excited to be part of.
<v Ira Glass>他们在交谈中也会说“我们是宾夕法尼亚州立大学”，以强调观点。他们是这个俱乐部的一部分，他们为此感到非常兴奋。

00:28:26.870 --> 00:28:45.350
<v Ira Glass>In 2009, we were there for the last home game of the season, exactly two years and one week ago. Penn State was 44,000 students at the time. To describe the level of drinking and partying and football fandom, at one point in the show, I was joined in the studio, back in 2009, by Sarah Koenig, who you heard earlier in today's show.
<v Ira Glass>2009年，我们在赛季的最后一场主场比赛时在那里，就在两年前的一周前。宾夕法尼亚州立大学当时有4.4万名学生。为了描述饮酒、派对和橄榄球粉丝的热度，在节目中的某个时刻，2009年时，我曾与今天节目中出现的莎拉·柯尼格一起在录音室里。

00:28:45.350 --> 00:29:15.290
<v Sarah Koenig>You're right. A couple of weeks before you guys came to State College, there was this huge freak snowstorm, and they had to cancel tailgating for the first time anyone could remember because the conditions were so dangerous by the field. But it didn't stop anyone. I saw people tailgating in their yards, on their front lawns-- even in my neighborhood, I went into a parking garage and found all these people on the fourth floor, which was really strange to see. I mean, it's a parking garage, so there's dirty cars everywhere, the ceiling is low, it's cold, it's dark.
<v Sarah Koenig>你是对的。在你们去州立学院的前几周，发生了一场巨大的怪异暴风雪，他们不得不取消了有史以来第一次尾门派对（tailgating），因为场地条件太危险了。但它并没有阻止任何人。我看到人们在他们的院子里、在前草坪上尾门聚会——甚至在我住的社区，我走进了一个停车场，发现四楼全是这些人，这真的很奇怪。我的意思是，这是一个停车场，所以到处都是脏兮兮的车，天花板很低，又冷又暗。

00:29:15.290 --> 00:29:23.620
<v Sarah Koenig>At the end of one row of cars, there were these two tables pushed together in an L-shape with tablecloths and all kinds of food and a couple birthday cakes.
<v Sarah Koenig>在车行尽头，有两张桌子拼在一起呈L形，铺着桌布，摆满了各种食物和几个生日蛋糕。

00:29:23.620 --> 00:29:24.630
<v Sarah Koenig>Whose birthday is this?
<v Sarah Koenig>这是谁的生日？

00:29:24.630 --> 00:29:29.010
<v Female Student 1>It's my birthday, it's my birthday, my 21st birthday.
<v Female Student 1>是我的生日，是我的生日，我21岁的生日。

00:29:29.010 --> 00:29:30.220
<v Sarah Koenig>Happy birthday.
<v Sarah Koenig>生日快乐。

00:29:30.220 --> 00:29:31.050
<v Female Student 1>Thank you.
<v Female Student 1>谢谢。

00:29:31.050 --> 00:29:34.000
<v Sarah Koenig>And what are you doing on the fourth floor of a parking garage?
<v Sarah Koenig>那你为什么在停车场的四楼？

00:29:34.000 --> 00:29:37.540
<v Female Student 1>I'm getting drunk and tailgating.
<v Female Student 1>我正在喝得烂醉，进行尾门聚会。

00:29:37.540 --> 00:29:40.120
<v Sarah Koenig>Describe your headgear there.
<v Sarah Koenig>描述一下你的头饰。

00:29:40.120 --> 00:29:45.510
<v Female Student 1>It's a crown with a 21 that's on fire, and it says, "Where's the bar?"
<v Female Student 1>这是一个写着“酒吧在哪？”的皇冠，上面还有一个着火的21数字。

00:29:45.510 --> 00:29:47.210
<v Sarah Koenig>So who brought all this food?
<v Sarah Koenig>那这些食物是谁带来的？

00:29:47.210 --> 00:29:51.870
<v Female Student 1>My mom and dad. Because they love me, and they're the best.
<v Female Student 1>我的爸爸妈妈。因为他们爱我，他们是最好的。

00:29:51.870 --> 00:30:00.850
<v Sarah Koenig>About a half dozen older people were there, too, her parents and some of their friends. And there were a bunch of other tailgates happening around us, and one couple was having their 40th wedding anniversary.
<v Sarah Koenig>大约有半打老年人也在那里，她的父母和他们的一些朋友。我们周围还有一堆其他的尾门聚会，有一对夫妇正在庆祝结婚40周年。

00:30:00.850 --> 00:30:02.870
<v Ira Glass>Wow. In the garage?
<v Ira Glass>哇。在停车场？

00:30:02.870 --> 00:30:09.740
<v Sarah Koenig>In the garage, yeah. At one point, a woman probably in her 50s walked up and handed the birthday girl a red plastic cup.
<v Sarah Koenig>在停车场，是的。有一次，一位大概50多岁的女士走过来，递给寿星一个红色塑料杯。

00:30:09.740 --> 00:30:13.210
<v Female Student 1>Aw, thank you! What is this?
<v Female Student 1>啊，谢谢！这是什么？

00:30:13.210 --> 00:30:15.420
<v Woman 1>[INAUDIBLE]
<v Woman 1>[听不清]

00:30:15.420 --> 00:30:17.500
<v Female Student 1>Is there alcohol in it?
<v Female Student 1>里面有酒精吗？

00:30:17.500 --> 00:30:20.550
<v Woman 1>Most definitely.
<v Woman 1>那是肯定的。

00:30:20.550 --> 00:30:32.210
<v Female Student 1>Oh my god, this is the strongest drink I've ever had in my life! They just brought me a really strong alcoholic lemonade.
<v Female Student 1>天哪，这是我这辈子喝过最浓的饮料！他们刚刚给了我一杯酒精浓度很高的柠檬水。

00:30:32.210 --> 00:30:33.640
<v Sarah Koenig>Is that your daughter? Are you the mom?
<v Sarah Koenig>那是你女儿吗？你是妈妈吗？

00:30:33.640 --> 00:30:36.570
<v Woman 1>No, no. We're parked over there.
<v Woman 1>不，不。我们停在那边。

00:30:36.570 --> 00:30:38.110
<v Sarah Koenig>You just saw that this is happening.
<v Sarah Koenig>你只是看到这里在发生这些事。

00:30:38.110 --> 00:30:40.580
<v Woman 1>Yes.
<v Woman 1>是的。

00:30:40.580 --> 00:30:43.120
<v Man 1>We've got to keep ourselves entertained somehow, right?
<v Man 1>我们必须得找点乐子来消遣一下，对吧？

00:30:43.120 --> 00:30:45.530
<v Sarah Koenig>Watch the 21-year-old get plastered, that's the entertainment?
<v Sarah Koenig>看着那个21岁的女孩喝得酩酊大醉，这就是娱乐活动？

00:30:45.530 --> 00:30:49.110
<v Man 1>Exactly, exactly.
<v Man 1>正是，正是。

00:30:49.110 --> 00:30:59.760
<v Female Student 1>Thank you so much for the drink!
<v Female Student 1>太感谢你的饮料了！

00:30:59.760 --> 00:31:15.470
<v Ira Glass>At tailgate parties you see just how deeply embedded drinking is in life at Penn State.  It's entire families. It's several generations together. It's outdoor public drinking, which gives you this feeling when you're there that the whole world is drinking. Probably because there's so much of it.
<v Ira Glass>在尾门派对上，你可以看到饮酒在宾夕法尼亚州立大学的生活中是多么根深蒂固。这是整个家庭。这是几代人在一起。这是露天的公共饮酒，这让你在那里有一种全世界都在喝酒的感觉。可能是因为酒实在太多了。

00:31:15.470 --> 00:31:33.750
<v Ira Glass>In 2009, when we were there, Penn State had largest stadium in North America. Holds over 100,000 people. So the tailgates are these huge masses of humanity spread out as far as you can see, all around the stadium, starting at 8 in the morning, starting really early, which is when these alums got here. They talked to one of the producers, Aaron Scott.
<v Ira Glass>2009年我们在那里时，宾夕法尼亚州立大学拥有北美最大的体育场。能容纳超过10万人。所以尾门聚会是巨大的人群，绵延到你所能看到的尽头，在体育场周围，早上8点就开始了，开始得很早，这些校友就是那时候到的。他们和制片人之一亚伦·斯科特（Aaron Scott）谈过。

00:31:33.750 --> 00:31:38.420
<v Aaron Scott>So you went to bed, three or four. You got up, and you're drinking again.
<v Aaron Scott>所以你睡了三四个小时。你起床了，又开始喝了。

00:31:38.420 --> 00:31:38.960
<v Girls>Yes.
<v Girls>是的。

00:31:38.960 --> 00:31:40.510
<v Aaron Scott>Is it ever too early to drink?
<v Aaron Scott>喝酒会嫌太早吗？

00:31:40.510 --> 00:31:42.120
<v Girls>No, never.
<v Girls>不，从来不会。

00:31:42.120 --> 00:31:49.890
<v Ira Glass>Eight in the morning means, by the way, that if it's a night game, they will tailgate for 12 hours, in the cold, in the freezing cold sometimes, before kickoff.
<v Ira Glass>顺便说一句，早上八点意味着如果是夜间比赛，他们会在寒冷中，有时是在冰冷的严寒中，在开球前进行12小时的尾门聚会。

00:31:49.890 --> 00:31:54.130
<v Sarah Koenig>I talked to a bunch of alums at a tailgate about what would happen if they weren't allowed to drink it all.
<v Sarah Koenig>我和一群校友谈论过如果他们不允许喝酒会发生什么。

00:31:54.130 --> 00:32:05.150
<v Mark Johnson>I think there would be a revolt. I think there would be a huge, huge pushback from the alumni that donate a lot of money to school to say that you can't have alcohol to tailgate.
<v Mark Johnson>我想会发生暴动。我想那些为学校捐赠大量金钱的校友会进行巨大的阻力，声称不允许在尾门聚会时喝酒。

00:32:05.150 --> 00:32:07.970
<v Sarah Koenig>That's Mark Johnson, class of 1977.
<v Sarah Koenig>那是马克·约翰逊（Mark Johnson），1977届学生。

00:32:07.970 --> 00:32:18.590
<v Mark Johnson>It's just part of the culture. I mean, that's why this school sells 110,000 tickets for every-- just, it's a part of it. It's just part of that tradition.
<v Mark Johnson>这只是文化的一部分。我的意思是，这就是为什么这所学校每场比赛都能卖出11万张票……这只是其中的一部分。这只是传统的一部分。

00:32:18.590 --> 00:32:39.620
<v Ira Glass>Usually pounding down drinks at eight in the morning can be kind of sad. But the main thing about this kind of drinking, where it's tied in with tradition, and football, and family, and overwhelming sense of school spirit, is that it feels incredibly wholesome. The whole campus feels like a Chevy commercial. It's welcoming people.  It's friendly. It's hard to resist.
<v Ira Glass>通常在早上八点就开始酗酒可能看起来有点悲伤。但这种与传统、橄榄球、家庭和强烈的学校精神联系在一起的饮酒的主要特征是，它感觉非常健康。整个校园感觉就像雪佛兰的广告。它是受欢迎的。它是友好的。很难抗拒。

00:32:39.620 --> 00:32:44.790
<v Megan Klock>You could go to any tailgate here, any Penn State tailgate, and you are just welcome.
<v Megan Klock>你可以去这里的任何一个尾门聚会，任何一个宾夕法尼亚州立大学的尾门聚会，你都会受到欢迎。

00:32:44.790 --> 00:32:46.580
<v Ira Glass>Here's a junior named Megan Klock.
<v Ira Glass>这是大三学生梅根·克洛克（Megan Klock）。

00:32:46.580 --> 00:33:04.670
<v Megan Klock>I mean, you could go up to someone that's cooking at a grill, and be like, hey, can I have a burger? And they're like, oh, sure. So it's like endless food. Everyone's friendly with everyone. Just the atmosphere here is-- I have never experienced anything like Penn State atmosphere.
<v Megan Klock>我的意思是，你可以走到一个在烤架上做饭的人身边，说，嘿，我能来个汉堡吗？他们会说，哦，当然。所以就像有无穷无尽的食物。每个人都对彼此很友好。这里的氛围就是……我从未体验过像宾夕法尼亚州立大学这样的氛围。

00:33:04.670 --> 00:33:09.940
<v Ira Glass>Sarah met this next guy, Peter, drinking outside the stadium with his friends before the game. He's from Slovakia.
<v Ira Glass>莎拉在比赛前遇见了下一个人，彼得，在体育场外和朋友喝酒。他来自斯洛伐克。

00:33:09.940 --> 00:33:48.320
<v Peter>I came to US, and I started watching baseball and football, and I didn't understand at all the rules. It was just a big blah to me. And I didn't get my football tickets my freshman year, but I was able to go in one or two games. And I guess I started to like the atmosphere that is surrounded by the football. And it kind of reminded me of home, where everyone is welcoming, everyone's a big family, and no matter where you're from, you root for the same team. It made me feel like I'm home again and surrounded by friends. And so I just love it now. I heart [UNINTELLIGIBLE]. I'm getting emotional.
<v Peter>我来到美国，开始看棒球和橄榄球，我一点也没弄懂规则。对我来说那简直是一团糟。我大一时没买到橄榄球门票，但我能进去看一两场比赛。我想我开始喜欢橄榄球周围的那种氛围了。它让我想起了家乡，每个人都很受欢迎，每个人都是一个大家庭，无论你来自哪里，你都支持同一支球队。它让我感觉像是又回到了家，周围都是朋友。所以我现在就是爱它。我爱[听不清]。我都感动了。

00:33:48.320 --> 00:33:58.520
<v Ira Glass>This sense-- that we're a family, we're about football, everybody in the school is one-- it spills over from the football games into the whole weekend. It's a week, actually.
<v Ira Glass>这种感觉——我们是一个家庭，我们是橄榄球，学校里的每个人都是一体的——它从橄榄球比赛溢出到整个周末。其实是一个星期。

00:33:58.520 --> 00:34:22.430
<v Ira Glass>It's Friday night, the night before the football game, outside East Hall, one of the big freshmen dorms. And dozens of kids are lined up to the free shuttle buses that are going to take them across campus to the frats and parties on the other side. Two of our producers, Jane Feltes and Lisa Pollak, noticed that all the girls wore these ratty-looking hoodies and jackets over their dress-up clothes. Somebody told them that word for this is "frackets." They went up to some girls to confirm that.
<v Ira Glass>这是周五晚上，橄榄球比赛的前一晚，在东大厅外，这是大型大一宿舍楼之一。几十个孩子排队等候免费的穿梭巴士，这些巴士将带他们穿过校园去另一边的兄弟会和派对现场。我们的两位制片人简·费尔特斯（Jane Feltes）和丽莎·波拉克（Lisa Pollak）注意到所有的女孩在她们精心打扮的衣服外面都穿着这些看起来破烂不堪的连帽衫和夹克。有人告诉她们这个词叫“frackets”。她们走到一些女孩身边确认了一下。

00:34:22.430 --> 00:34:24.860
<v Lisa Pollak>Have you guys heard of something called frackets?
<v Lisa Pollak>你们听说过叫“frackets”的东西吗？

00:34:24.860 --> 00:34:27.780
<v Female Student 2>Oh yeah, that's what we're wearing.
<v Female Student 2>哦，是的，这就是我们穿的东西。

00:34:27.780 --> 00:34:29.440
<v Female Student 3>Oh, we have a pro in frackets.
<v Female Student 3>哦，我们有穿frackets的专家。

00:34:29.440 --> 00:34:37.150
<v Female Student 4>A fracket, by definition, is a jacket made for frats. It's basically a crappy jacket that's cheap and you don't care about.
<v Female Student 4>Fracket，按照定义，是专为兄弟会聚会（frat）做的夹克。它基本上是一件便宜、破旧的夹克，你并不在意它。

00:34:37.150 --> 00:34:41.150
<v Female Student 2>Yeah. Because they get lost. They get, like, puked on. You don't want that back.
<v Female Student 2>是的。因为它会丢。它会被，比如，吐在上面。你可不想要回它。

00:34:41.150 --> 00:34:43.940
<v Female Student 5>You don't want it back. Just lose it, yeah.
<v Female Student 5>你不想要回它。就是丢掉它，是的。

00:34:43.940 --> 00:34:46.940
<v Ira Glass>Jane rode on the bus with the freshmen. She joins me now.
<v Ira Glass>简坐着大一新生坐的巴士。她现在加入了我的谈话。

00:34:46.940 --> 00:34:58.640
<v Jane Feltes>So that girl, her name's Chuy, she's from Saudi Arabia. When I met her and her friend Megna, they were already on their second loop around campus. They were kind of drunk and they'd missed their stop the first time. And on this bus, everyone keeps breaking into song.
<v Jane Feltes>那个女孩，她叫楚伊（Chuy），她来自沙特阿拉伯。当我见到她和她的朋友梅格娜（Megna）时，她们已经在校园里兜第二圈了。她们有点醉了，第一次还坐过站了。在这辆巴士上，每个人都在唱歌。

00:34:58.640 --> 00:35:03.390
<v Jane Feltes>[STUDENTS SINGING]
<v Jane Feltes>[学生唱歌]

00:35:03.390 --> 00:35:05.120
<v Jane Feltes>What is happening?
<v Jane Feltes>发生什么事了？

00:35:05.120 --> 00:35:08.540
<v Female Student 2>This is the drunk bus. Good timing.
<v Female Student 2>这是醉鬼巴士。时机正好。

00:35:08.540 --> 00:35:13.580
<v Female Student 5>Oh my god. Is this the same stop where we found out that we were late?
<v Female Student 5>噢，天哪。这是我们发现我们迟到的那一站吗？

00:35:13.580 --> 00:35:43.310
<v Ira Glass>Penn State's administration does a pretty thorough job surveying students to track how much they're drinking. Surveys show that every Friday and every Saturday night, 75% of the school drinks. That's over 30,000 people, an average of four and half drinks per person. And the students drink those drinks in about three hours, average. To put those numbers in perspective, binge drinking, or as it's been rebranded lately, high-risk or dangerous drinking, is defined as four drinks for a woman or five drinks for a man consumed in just two hours.
<v Ira Glass>宾夕法尼亚州立大学的行政部门在调查学生饮酒量方面做得相当彻底。调查显示，每个周五和周六晚上，75%的学生都在喝酒。那超过3万人，平均每人喝四杯半。而学生们在三小时内平均喝完这些酒。为了客观地看待这些数字，酗酒，或者像最近被重塑为高风险或危险饮酒，被定义为女性喝四杯，男性喝五杯，仅在两小时内喝完。

00:35:43.310 --> 00:35:51.520
<v Ira Glass>[STUDENTS SINGING]
<v Ira Glass>[学生唱歌]

00:35:51.520 --> 00:36:00.510
<v Ira Glass>And to get down into the details of Penn State's numbers, it works out to over half the students regularly binge drinking, which is just a little bit above the national average for college students.
<v Ira Glass>为了深入了解宾夕法尼亚州立大学的数字，超过一半的学生有规律地酗酒，这比大学生全国平均水平略高一点。

00:36:00.510 --> 00:36:06.840
<v Ira Glass>[STUDENTS SINGING]
<v Ira Glass>[学生唱歌]

00:36:06.840 --> 00:36:13.850
<v Ira Glass>Lots of students said something that was hard to argue with. When else are also they going to get a chance to do this? Like I was talking to a senior--
<v Ira Glass>很多学生说了些很难反驳的话。他们还有什么时候有机会做这种事？就像我和一位大四学生谈话时——

00:36:13.850 --> 00:36:18.600
<v Ira Glass>So what's the wildest thing you've seen at a party at this school?
<v Ira Glass>那么你在学校的派对上见过的最狂野的事情是什么？

00:36:18.600 --> 00:36:32.650
<v Male Student 5>Wildest thing at a party? Somebody streaking completely naked, and pretending to throw monkey feces as they were doing it. And that was me.
<v Male Student 5>派对上最狂野的事？有人一丝不挂地裸奔，并在做的时候假装扔猴子粪便。那个人就是我。

00:36:32.650 --> 00:36:33.310
<v Ira Glass>That was you?
<v Ira Glass>那是你？

00:36:33.310 --> 00:36:34.320
<v Male Student 5>That was me.
<v Male Student 5>那就是我。

00:36:34.320 --> 00:36:35.790
<v Ira Glass>When was that?
<v Ira Glass>那是什么时候？

00:36:35.790 --> 00:36:52.240
<v Male Student 5>Last week. I mean, you can only do that stuff now, when you're in college, you know? You can't do it-- I wouldn't be able to do it six months from now. It's college.
<v Male Student 5>上周。我的意思是，你只有在现在，当你上大学的时候，才能做这些事情，你知道吗？你不能在……六个月后我就做不到了。这是大学。

00:36:52.240 --> 00:37:22.670
<v Ira Glass>Yes, there's a downside to all this. In the surveys of students done by the school, a fourth of Penn State students say that drinking has caused them to miss class or get behind on the schoolwork. 15% say that they've been pushed, hit, or assaulted. 7% say that drinking led to what the survey called-- this is their euphemism-- quote, "an unwanted sexual experience." There actually isn't data at Penn State linking drinking and date rape. 6% said that they'd gotten into what you would call a real physical fight. And then there are crimes that you never hear of unless you live in a place like State College.
<v Ira Glass>是的，所有这一切都有负面影响。在学校对学生进行的调查中，四分之一的宾夕法尼亚州立大学学生说饮酒导致他们旷课或学习落后。15%的人说他们被推挤、殴打或侵犯过。7%的人说饮酒导致了他们所谓的——这是他们的委婉说法——引述“不想要的性体验”。宾夕法尼亚州立大学实际上并没有数据将饮酒与约会强奸联系起来。6%的人说他们卷入过所谓的真正的身体斗争。还有一些除非你住像州立学院这样的地方，否则你永远听不到的犯罪。

00:37:22.670 --> 00:37:47.840
<v Nina White>Our daughter came into the kitchen while we were in the midst of hurriedly getting everything done, and said something to the effect of, mommy, I want to play my room. Can you please get that man out of my bed? And I remember having said something to the effect-- there's no man in your bed. And she actually went away, and came back a few minutes later, so I guess she went up to check. And she comes down--there is a man in my bed, and I want to play in my room. Would you please get him out of there?
<v Nina White>当我们匆忙完成所有事情时，我们的女儿走进厨房，说了类似这样的话，妈妈，我想玩我的房间。你能把那个男人从我的床上弄走吗？我记得我说了类似——你的床上没有男人。她真的走开了，几分钟后又回来了，所以我想她去检查了。她下来说——我的床上有个男人，我想玩我的房间。你能把他弄走吗？

00:37:47.840 --> 00:37:59.360
<v Ira Glass>This is a long-time State College resident named Nina White, and the man on the bed, of course, was a college student who committed a surprisingly common crime in this town. He came to her house drunk, found a comfortable place, and fell asleep.
<v Ira Glass>这是一位在州立学院住了很久的居民，名叫妮娜·怀特（Nina White），当然，床上的男人是一个在镇上犯了令人惊讶的常见罪行的大学生。他喝醉了来到她家，找了一个舒服的地方，然后睡着了。

00:37:59.360 --> 00:38:20.320
<v Ira Glass>When we were at Penn State in 2009, it was the neighbors who had the biggest concerns about all the drinking because of incidents like this. And there was one of the neighbors in particular who got us interested in in all the partying as something to report on.  That neighbor was our colleague, This American Life producer Sarah Koenig, who, as I said, lives in State College. Lives there because her husband teaches at the school. And the episode that we did in 2009 began with us sitting on the porch.
<v Ira Glass>2009年我们在宾夕法尼亚州立大学时，正是邻居们最关心所有这些酗酒问题，因为发生了诸如此类的事件。并且有一位邻居特别让我们对所有的派对作为报道对象产生了兴趣。那位邻居是我们的同事，《This American Life》制片人莎拉·柯尼格，正如我所说，她住在州立学院。住在那里是因为她丈夫在学校教书。我们在2009年制作的那期节目以我们在门廊上坐着开始。

00:38:20.320 --> 00:38:34.690
<v Ira Glass>One, two, three. OK, Sarah, so it's ten of one on a Friday night in November, and we're sitting on your porch. And it seems pretty quiet.
<v Ira Glass>一、二、三。好了，莎拉，现在是11月一个周五晚上的凌晨12点50分，我们正坐在你的门廊上。看起来相当安静。

00:38:34.690 --> 00:38:36.300
<v Sarah Koenig>It's not that bad.
<v Sarah Koenig>没那么糟。

00:38:36.300 --> 00:38:54.490
<v Ira Glass>By day, I have to say, Sarah's neighborhood is like a college town that you would see in an old Hollywood film. It's beautiful professors' homes, built in the '20s and '30s, it's tree-lined streets, it's gardens. But by night, we'd been on that porch for five minutes when--
<v Ira Glass>白天，我不得不说，莎拉的社区就像你在旧好莱坞电影中看到的那种大学城。它是美丽的教授住宅，建于20世纪20和30年代，它是绿树成荫的街道，它是花园。但到了晚上，我们在那个门廊待了五分钟时——

00:38:54.490 --> 00:39:01.430
<v Sarah Koenig>They seem drunk. These kids seem drunk. This couple here, they're sort of staggering a bit. I think he's like holding her up or something.
<v Sarah Koenig>他们看起来喝醉了。这些孩子看起来喝醉了。这对夫妇在这里，他们有点摇摇晃晃。我想他像是扶着她之类的。

00:39:01.430 --> 00:39:05.110
<v Ira Glass>It's nine students walking down the middle of the street. Happens all the time.
<v Ira Glass>那是九个学生走在街中间。经常发生。

00:39:05.110 --> 00:39:20.090
<v Sarah Koenig>It's noisy, right? It's like it doesn't occur to them at all, I think, that there are people in these houses trying to sleep. And I kind of remember being that way in college, too, actually.
<v Sarah Koenig>很吵，对吧？就像他们完全没意识到，我想，这些房子里有人在试图睡觉。而且我有点记得在大学里自己也是那样，其实。

00:39:20.090 --> 00:39:21.530
<v Ira Glass>Yeah, I do too.
<v Ira Glass>是的，我也记得。

00:39:21.530 --> 00:39:26.300
<v Ira Glass>Six minutes later, there's another group of drunk students. One tosses something onto a lawn.
<v Ira Glass>六分钟后，又是另一群喝醉的学生。其中一个往草坪上扔了什么东西。

00:39:26.300 --> 00:39:36.830
<v Sarah Koenig>Can you guys pick up your trash, please?  Can you pick up your plate, your pizza plate? Please? People leave here. They pay no attention.
<v Sarah Koenig>你们能把垃圾捡起来吗，拜托？你们能捡起你们的盘子，你们的披萨盘吗？拜托？人们住在这里。他们根本不注意。

00:39:36.830 --> 00:39:41.560
<v Ira Glass>Five minutes after that, we hear this clattering from the alley that runs next to Sarah's house.
<v Ira Glass>五分钟后，我们听到从莎拉房子旁的小巷传来的撞击声。

00:39:41.560 --> 00:39:46.580
<v Sarah Koenig>Whoa, what was that? Actually, I'd kind of like to know what that was. What's going on?
<v Sarah Koenig>哇，那是什么？其实，我倒是挺想知道那是什么。发生什么事了？

00:39:46.580 --> 00:39:53.300
<v Ira Glass>We run down the alley, and two college boys run away as they see us coming. "Don't go back there, you could get raped," they yell to Sarah.
<v Ira Glass>我们跑进小巷，两个大学生看到我们过来就跑了。“别回那儿，你可能会被强奸，”他们对莎拉喊道。

00:39:53.300 --> 00:40:01.030
<v Sarah Koenig>See? OK. They just threw somebody's trash can, or like dropkicked it up in the air.
<v Sarah Koenig>看到了吧？好的。他们只是扔了某人的垃圾桶，或者像是把它踢到半空中。

00:40:01.030 --> 00:40:06.890
<v Ira Glass>12 minutes after that, from the other direction, we hear a scraping, and a loud rumbling, I guess.
<v Ira Glass>在那之后的12分钟，从另一个方向，我们听到了一阵刮擦声，还有一阵巨大的轰鸣声，我想。

00:40:06.890 --> 00:40:12.800
<v Sarah Koenig>That's somebody's property. That might have been a sign, actually.
<v Sarah Koenig>那是某人的财产。那可能是一个标志，其实。

00:40:12.800 --> 00:40:25.180
<v Ira Glass>I don't know how many street signs you need to hear dragged through your neighborhood before you could recognize the sound it makes from all the way around the corner. But apparently my colleague Sarah has seen whatever that number is. We go to investigate and find two guys.
<v Ira Glass>我不知道在你能识别出从角落转弯处发出的声音之前，你需要听到多少个路牌被拖过你的社区。但显然我的同事莎拉见过那个次数。我们去调查并发现了两个人。

00:40:25.180 --> 00:40:27.820
<v Ira Glass>So just describe what you've got here.
<v Ira Glass>所以描述一下你们这里有什么。

00:40:27.820 --> 00:40:31.170
<v Male Student 6>A stop sign. Nice big pole on it.
<v Male Student 6>一个停车标志。上面有个漂亮的杆子。

00:40:31.170 --> 00:40:36.350
<v Sarah Koenig>A stop sign. That's kind of a big thing when you see it up close, isn't it?
<v Sarah Koenig>一个停车标志。当你近距离观察它时，那是个大东西，不是吗？

00:40:36.350 --> 00:40:36.680
<v Male Student 6>Sorry.
<v Male Student 6>抱歉。

00:40:36.680 --> 00:40:39.140
<v Sarah Koenig>Where did you find this? Like, where does it belong?
<v Sarah Koenig>你们在哪里找到这个的？比如，它属于哪里？

00:40:39.140 --> 00:40:39.600
<v Male Student 6>I don't know.
<v Male Student 6>我不知道。

00:40:39.600 --> 00:40:40.530
<v Male Student 7>That is a good question.
<v Male Student 7>这是个好问题。

00:40:40.530 --> 00:40:43.510
<v Male Student 6>We just found it on a lawn. We found it on a lawn.
<v Male Student 6>我们只是在草坪上发现了它。我们在草坪上发现了它。

00:40:43.510 --> 00:40:44.240
<v Male Student 7>It was already ripped up.
<v Male Student 7>它已经被拔出来了。

00:40:44.240 --> 00:40:45.720
<v Male Student 6>Yeah, it was already [BLEEP] on someone's lawn.
<v Male Student 6>是的，它已经在某人的草坪上[被搞砸了]。

00:40:45.720 --> 00:40:48.770
<v Male Student 7>We just decided, hey, why not make use of and take it?
<v Male Student 7>我们只是决定，嘿，为什么不利用它并带走呢？

00:40:48.770 --> 00:40:53.680
<v Ira Glass>Of course, this story makes no sense at all. I inform the two guys that I don't need to know their names.
<v Ira Glass>当然，这个故事根本没有任何意义。我告诉那两个人我不需要知道他们的名字。

00:40:53.680 --> 00:40:56.510
<v Male Student 7>Well, in that case, we took it from the corner right there.
<v Male Student 7>好吧，如果是那样的话，我们是从那边的拐角处拿走的。

00:40:56.510 --> 00:40:57.940
<v Sarah Koenig>Which one?
<v Sarah Koenig>哪一个？

00:40:57.940 --> 00:41:00.780
<v Male Student 7>Where that car just turned.
<v Male Student 7>那辆车刚转过去的地方。

00:41:00.780 --> 00:41:02.020
<v Ira Glass>Pulled it out of the ground?
<v Ira Glass>从地里拔出来的？

00:41:02.020 --> 00:41:09.960
<v Male Student 7>Yeah, it was cemented in pretty well. Just get a little rocking back and forth between two people, comes right out.
<v Male Student 7>是的，它用水泥固定得相当好。只要两个人稍微来回摇晃一下，就出来了。

00:41:09.960 --> 00:41:12.190
<v Ira Glass>Did you guys come from like a house party somewhere?
<v Ira Glass>你们是从什么家庭聚会过来的吗？

00:41:12.190 --> 00:41:17.510
<v Male Student 7>Ah, no.  We were just hanging out with a bunch of friends, just drinking. Taking some shots.
<v Male Student 7>啊，不。我们只是和一群朋友待在一起，只是在喝酒。喝点酒。

00:41:17.510 --> 00:41:20.540
<v Ira Glass>Seems like kind of an academic distinction to me, but.
<v Ira Glass>对我来说这似乎是一个学术上的区别，但是。

00:41:20.540 --> 00:41:38.050
<v Ira Glass>With that, the other guy starts running with the street sign toward Garner Street, which is hard because it's attached to a seven-foot metal pole, and it's heavy. Sarah and I go back around the corner to her front yard. The evening is not over at all. Just in time to spot another student, this one coming out of her garden, Sarah assumes, from the bushes back there.
<v Ira Glass>随之，另一个人开始带着路牌向加纳街（Garner Street）跑去，这很困难，因为它连接着一根七英尺高的金属杆，而且很重。莎拉和我转过街角回到她的前院。夜晚根本还没结束。刚好赶上看到另一个学生，这个学生从她的花园里出来，莎拉推测，从后面的灌木丛里。

00:41:38.050 --> 00:41:39.560
<v Male Student 8>I didn't pee.
<v Male Student 8>我没撒尿。

00:41:39.560 --> 00:41:40.530
<v Sarah Koenig>Really?
<v Sarah Koenig>真的吗？

00:41:40.530 --> 00:41:41.380
<v Male Student 8>I never pee.
<v Male Student 8>我从来不撒尿。

00:41:41.380 --> 00:41:41.770
<v Sarah Koenig>Really?
<v Sarah Koenig>真的吗？

00:41:41.770 --> 00:41:43.430
<v Male Student 8>I was just sitting down.
<v Male Student 8>我只是坐下来。

00:41:43.430 --> 00:41:51.660
<v Ira Glass>He goes, and it's not a minute later that Sarah points towards the alley where there are three girls in miniskirts, under streetlights, fully visible.
<v Ira Glass>他走了，没过一分钟莎拉就指向小巷，那里有三个穿着超短裙的女孩，在路灯下，完全可见。

00:41:51.660 --> 00:41:55.120
<v Sarah Koenig>Peeing, peeing, peeing, peeing, peeing.
<v Sarah Koenig>撒尿，撒尿，撒尿，撒尿，撒尿。

00:41:55.120 --> 00:41:57.180
<v Ira Glass>One girl hikes up her skirt.
<v Ira Glass>一个女孩提起她的裙子。

00:41:57.180 --> 00:41:58.470
<v Ira Glass>Wait, step back, step back, step back.
<v Ira Glass>等等，退后，退后，退后。

00:41:58.470 --> 00:42:08.050
<v Sarah Koenig>OK. But they're peeing in my yard. They're peeing in my yard. That's my car, OK, and three feet back is a girl's white ass, peeing.
<v Sarah Koenig>好的。但她们在我的院子里撒尿。她们在我的院子里撒尿。那是我的车，好的，三英尺后面是一个女孩的白屁股，撒尿。

00:42:08.050 --> 00:42:17.690
<v Sarah Koenig>She saw we saw her. She stopped. You know, that might be why the plants grow really, really well in that spot. I'm just realizing.
<v Sarah Koenig>她看到我们看到她了。她停下了。你知道，那可能就是为什么植物在那个点长得特别特别好的原因。我刚刚意识到。

00:42:17.690 --> 00:42:24.490
<v Ira Glass>Sarah's caught other groups of girls peeing in that same spot. Once she heard a girl say, "This is a good place. I go here all the time."
<v Ira Glass>莎拉抓到过其他几群女孩在同一个地方撒尿。有一次她听到一个女孩说，“这是一个好地方。我一直来这里。”

00:42:24.490 --> 00:42:29.320
<v Sarah Koenig>They're so embarrassed. There's like muffled giggling happening across the street.
<v Sarah Koenig>她们太尴尬了。马路对面传来了闷声的咯咯笑声。

00:42:29.320 --> 00:42:52.310
<v Ira Glass>The weekend that we recorded this, back in 2009, Penn State was in fact the country's number one party school, named by people who name the number one party school in our country, the Princeton Review. And this is apparently what it means to be the number one party school in the country. From the moment that Sarah and I turned on the tape recorder to the moment that she sent those girls looking for somebody else's yard to pee in-- total time, 34 minutes. It's 1:30 in the morning now.
<v Ira Glass>我们录制这个周末时，2009年时，宾夕法尼亚州立大学确实是全国第一派对学校，由命名我们国家第一派对学校的人们评选，即普林斯顿评论（Princeton Review）。这就是成为全国第一派对学校意味着什么。从莎拉和我打开录音机的那一刻起，到她送那些女孩寻找别人院子去撒尿的那一刻——总时间，34分钟。现在是凌晨1点半。

00:42:52.310 --> 00:43:12.630
<v Sarah Koenig>The fact that we saw that much mayhem sort of going on at one corner-- so that means it's, multiply that by this entire neighborhood and other neighborhoods around. People are peeing everywhere, garbage cans are getting kicked, stop signs are getting pulled out of the road, people are littering-- you know.
<v Sarah Koenig>我们在一个街角看到那么多混乱在发生——所以这意味着，乘以整个社区和其他周围的社区。人们到处在撒尿，垃圾桶被踢，停车标志被拔出道路，人们在乱扔垃圾——你知道的。

00:43:12.630 --> 00:43:47.600
<v Ira Glass>The Princeton Review chooses the number one party school from online student surveys. 120,000 students at 371 schools around the country answered these questions. How widely used are beer, alcohol, marijuana, and other drugs or your school? How big is frat life at your school? And finally, how many hours do you study each day? The students aren't randomly selected, so it's not, strictly speaking, a scientific survey. But every year, Penn State ranks very high. In the last five years, it's been the number three party school twice. It's been the number seven party school in the country, it's been the number nine party school in the country, and of course, number one party school in 2009 the year we were there.
<v Ira Glass>普林斯顿评论从在线学生调查中选择第一派对学校。全国371所学校的12万名学生回答了这些问题。你们学校啤酒、酒精、大麻和其他药物使用有多广泛？你们学校兄弟会生活有多庞大？最后，你们每天学习几个小时？学生们不是随机抽样的，所以严格来说，这不是科学调查。但每年，宾夕法尼亚州立大学排名都很高。过去五年里，它两次成为第三派对学校。它曾是全国第七派对学校，曾是全国第九派对学校，当然，2009年我们在那里时是第一派对学校。

00:43:47.600 --> 00:44:24.470
<v Ira Glass>That year, a freshman named Joe Dado died in an alcohol-related incident in September, just a month into the school year. He was 18. In the episode that we did back then, Sarah Koenig did a big story about the university's response to this incident and to the level of alcohol consumption at the school in general. When these kinds of deaths have happened at other schools-- Colorado State, MIT, Indiana University, UVA-- administrations have used the deaths to push for change. Not at Penn State. Nobody used the occasion to urge the students to stop drinking so much. Instead, the message from the administration was, if you're going to get plastered, do it more responsibly.
<v Ira Glass>那一年，一个叫乔·达多（Joe Dado）的新生在九月，即学年开始仅一个月后，死于一起与酒精有关的事件。他18岁。在那期节目中，莎拉·柯尼格做了一个关于学校对这一事件以及学校总体酒精消耗水平的反应的大型报道。当这种死亡在其他学校发生时——科罗拉多州立大学、麻省理工学院、印第安纳大学、弗吉尼亚大学——行政部门利用死亡来推动改变。在宾夕法尼亚州立大学没有。没有人利用这个机会敦促学生停止酗酒。相反，行政部门的信息是，如果你要喝得酩酊大醉，请更负责任地做。

00:44:24.470 --> 00:44:39.760
<v Ira Glass>As we noted at the time, maybe the administration figured that this message had a better chance of sticking. And it did stick. The lesson students took-- we heard this from students that we talked to-- was not the Dado drank too much, but that he would have been alive if only he hadn't gone off by himself.
<v Ira Glass>正如我们当时所指出的，也许行政部门认为这个信息更有可能被采纳。确实采纳了。学生们学到的教训——我们从谈话的学生那里听到的——不是达多喝得太多，而是如果他没有独自走开，他本来会活下来。

00:44:39.760 --> 00:45:00.820
<v Ira Glass>Sarah talked in the show about how the University's president, Graham Spanier, came in 15 years before that as a crusader against binge drinking who was not shy about calling alcohol use the school's biggest problem, and he got pushback for that. From alumni, from university trustees. He was booed by students at a football game. And he stopped hammering the topic so publicly.
<v Ira Glass>莎拉在节目中谈到了大学校长格雷厄姆·斯帕尼尔是如何在15年前作为一个反对酗酒的斗士进来的，他不害羞于称酒精使用是学校最大的问题，他为此受到了反击。来自校友，来自大学受托人。他在橄榄球比赛中被学生们嘘声。他停止如此公开地抨击这个话题。

00:45:00.820 --> 00:45:10.190
<v Ira Glass>Here's from that story that Sarah did back in 2009. You hear, she actually goes to Spanier.  And here's Spanier in this clip. He was fired last week after the child sex abuse scandal broke.
<v Ira Glass>这是莎拉在2009年做的那个故事里的片段。你听到她实际上去找了斯帕尼尔。这是片段中的斯帕尼尔。他上周在性虐待丑闻爆发后被解雇了。

00:45:10.190 --> 00:45:34.510
<v Sarah Koenig>What you hear all the time around here, off the record, from local residents, from faculty, from other administrators, is that Spanier really can't or won't do anything more draconian about the drinking problem here-- make fraternities dry, say, or curb tailgating-- because politically, he simply cannot risk alienating those sacred cows Simms is talking about. Especially alumni and athletics, meaning football, which brings in enormous money.
<v Sarah Koenig>你在这里一直听到的，非正式的，来自当地居民，来自教职员工，来自其他行政人员，是斯帕尼尔真的不能或不愿对这里的酗酒问题采取更严厉的行动——比如让兄弟会禁酒，或者限制尾门聚会——因为从政治上讲，他根本无法冒险疏远Simms所说的那些神圣母牛。尤其是校友和体育项目，意味着橄榄球，它带来了巨大的金钱。

00:45:34.510 --> 00:45:50.340
<v Sarah Koenig>Tens of thousands of alumni come to football games, rent skyboxes. The only places in the stadium where you can drink, by the way. They arrange their lives around the season. It's a big part of why they give money to the school. Last year, alums and other donors give $182 million.
<v Sarah Koenig>成千上万的校友来橄榄球比赛，租用豪华包厢。顺便说一句，这是体育场里唯一可以喝酒的地方。他们围绕着赛季安排他们的生活。这是他们向学校捐钱的重要原因。去年，校友和其他捐赠者捐赠了1.82亿美元。

00:45:50.340 --> 00:45:56.840
<v Sarah Koenig>I asked Spanier if there was something he needed to be careful about here, in terms of the connection between football and donors and booze.
<v Sarah Koenig>我问斯帕尼尔，在橄榄球与捐赠者和酒的关系方面，他是否需要小心。

00:45:56.840 --> 00:46:20.340
<v Graham Spanier>No. I don't buy that at all. That's just speculation that you hear from people, like, oh, that must be part of the issue. They would never go after the alumni. The fact is, what happens with six or seven home games is not the heart of this issue here. We're talking about the weekend in, weekend out, every day of the week throughout the year kind of issue.
<v Graham Spanier>不。我完全不买账。那只是你从人们那里听到的猜测，比如，哦，那一定是问题的一部分。他们永远不会对付校友。事实是，六七场主场比赛发生的事情不是这里问题的核心。我们谈论的是周末、周末，一年中每天那种问题。

00:46:20.340 --> 00:46:40.470
<v Sarah Koenig>When this comes up, it's sort of like, someone will say, well, why don't they just make tailgating dry? No alcohol on University property? Is that kind of thing ever even brought up as like a, we just want to signal a culture change, we're just going to make a big bold move and say, we're going to become a different kind of school now. Is that ever, like, on the table?
<v Sarah Koenig>当这种情况出现时，有点像，有人会说，嗯，为什么他们不直接让尾门聚会禁酒？大学财产上没有酒精？那种事情有没有被作为一种，我们只是想发出文化改变的信号，我们只是要做出一个重大的勇敢举措，说，我们要成为一种不同的学校了。这种情况是否在桌面上讨论过？

00:46:40.470 --> 00:47:14.990
<v Graham Spanier>No, I don't think so. I can't envision telling alumni of legal age that they can't drink on a football weekend, and seeing that that's going to change the problem of alcohol consumption among underage students on college campuses. I don't think those two things are really tied together.
<v Graham Spanier>不，我不这么认为。我无法想象告诉达到法定年龄的校友他们不能在橄榄球周末喝酒，并看到那会改变大学生酗酒的问题。我不认为这两件事真的联系在一起。

00:47:14.990 --> 00:47:26.150
<v Ira Glass>How far would administrators go to protect the football program? Of course, that's at the heart of the current scandal. And with football so central to Penn State's fundraising, it's easy to see why they would protect it.
<v Ira Glass>行政部门会走多远来保护橄榄球项目？当然，这是当前丑闻的核心。由于橄榄球对宾夕法尼亚州立大学的筹款如此核心，很容易理解为什么他们会保护它。

00:47:26.150 --> 00:48:05.010
<v Ira Glass>A Penn State professor named Michael Berube wrote an op-ed in the New York Times this week about how football built Penn State into the school it is today. He talks about how dramatically the school has improved since Coach Joe Paterno first arrived decades ago, how Paterno endowed the professorship he holds, and lists the academic departments at Penn State-- English, Sociology, Anthropology-- that are now rated among the top ten university programs by the National Research Council. He notes that after Joe Paterno's first national championship, Paterno declared that the school had to improve its library, because quote, "You can't have a great university without a great library." Paterno and his wife led the capital campaign. The new library building is named for them.
<v Ira Glass>一位宾夕法尼亚州立大学教授名叫迈克尔·贝鲁贝（Michael Berube），本周在《纽约时报》发表了一篇评论文章，讲述了橄榄球是如何将宾夕法尼亚州立大学建设成今天的学校的。他谈到自从几十年前乔·帕特诺教练第一次到来以来，学校是如何戏剧性地改善的，帕特诺如何资助了他所持有的教授席位，并列出了宾夕法尼亚州立大学的学术部门——英语、社会学、人类学——现在被国家研究委员会评为前十名大学项目。他指出，在乔·帕特诺第一次全国冠军赛后，帕特诺宣称学校必须改善其图书馆，因为引述：“没有伟大的图书馆，你就不能拥有伟大的大学。”帕特诺和他的妻子领导了资本运动。新的图书馆大楼以他们的名字命名。

00:48:05.010 --> 00:48:24.710
<v Ira Glass>When Penn State football has a winning season, enrollment goes up. Somebody who's tried to quantify just what football means to Penn State financially is Philadelphia Inquirer reporter Frank Fitzpatrick, whose 2000 series on the business of football at Penn State was nominated for a Pulitzer Prize. I reached him in State College this week, where he was writing a story about the current scandal.
<v Ira Glass>当宾夕法尼亚州立大学橄榄球队有一个获胜赛季时，入学人数就会上升。一位试图量化橄榄球对宾夕法尼亚州立大学财务意义的人是《费城问询报》（Philadelphia Inquirer）记者弗兰克·菲茨帕特里克（Frank Fitzpatrick），他2000年关于宾夕法尼亚州立大学橄榄球商业的系列报道被提名普利策奖。本周我在州立学院联系了他，他当时正在写关于当前丑闻的故事。

00:48:24.710 --> 00:48:32.940
<v Frank Fitzpatrick>You know, without football it's hard to imagine what this place would look like. Football has really been the engine driving the growth here.
<v Frank Fitzpatrick>你知道，没有橄榄球，很难想象这个地方会是什么样子。橄榄球确实一直是推动这里发展的引擎。

00:48:32.940 --> 00:48:34.720
<v Ira Glass>How much money does Penn State football bring in?
<v Ira Glass>宾夕法尼亚州立大学橄榄球带来了多少钱？

00:48:34.720 --> 00:48:52.630
<v Frank Fitzpatrick>Well, I believe in 2010, they brought in, I think it was $72 million. They had costs of, I believe, $19 million. So that's a profit of $53 million, which is a profit margin that most Fortune 500 companies would be envious of.
<v Frank Fitzpatrick>好吧，我相信在2010年，他们带来了，我想是7200万美元。他们有成本，我相信是1900万美元。所以那是5300万美元的利润，这是大多数《财富》500强公司都会嫉妒的利润率。

00:48:52.630 --> 00:48:57.600
<v Ira Glass>What happens at $53 million profit? Where does it go?
<v Ira Glass>5300万美元利润发生了什么？它去哪儿了？

00:48:57.600 --> 00:49:19.890
<v Frank Fitzpatrick>Well, it goes to fund the other 28 or 29 teams here at Penn State. The entire cost of these teams is carried by the revenue that the football team generates. Penn State is fortunate in that they're able to raise enough money to support athletics. At most schools in this country, they run an annual average deficit of maybe $8 million to $10 million a year.
<v Frank Fitzpatrick>好吧，它去资助宾夕法尼亚州立大学的其他28或29支球队。这些球队的全部成本由橄榄球队产生的收入承担。宾夕法尼亚州立大学很幸运，因为他们能够筹集足够的钱来支持体育运动。在这个国家的多数学校，他们每年平均运行大约800万到1000万美元的赤字。

00:49:19.890 --> 00:49:28.740
<v Ira Glass>You were saying that $53 million is the profit in a year from football at Penn State. How does that compare to Penn State budget? Like, is that a big chunk of it?
<v Ira Glass>你刚才说5300万美元是宾夕法尼亚州立大学橄榄球一年的利润。这与宾夕法尼亚州立大学预算相比如何？比如，这是一个很大的部分吗？

00:49:28.740 --> 00:49:32.130
<v Frank Fitzpatrick>Their overall budget for the school is probably like $4 billion.
<v Frank Fitzpatrick>他们学校的总预算可能像40亿美元。

00:49:32.130 --> 00:49:37.390
<v Ira Glass>It just doesn't seem like that much. Like, it doesn't seem like it would have that much influence.
<v Ira Glass>看起来好像没那么多。比如，看起来它不会有那么大的影响力。

00:49:37.390 --> 00:50:15.010
<v Frank Fitzpatrick>Well, what you've got to remember is that football and Joe Paterno are the face of this university. I mean, that's the marketing of your university. And you go around campus and look at the names of the buildings and the people that have donated money here, and they're either ex-football players, people who have somehow been associated with Joe over the years. So I mean, if you don't have a successful football team, if you don't have a team that's generating revenue and victories, you're quickly going to find yourself with decreased admissions, with lower contributions.
<v Frank Fitzpatrick>好吧，你必须记住的是，橄榄球和乔·帕特诺是这所大学的面孔。我的意思是，那是你大学的营销。你在校园里走走，看看建筑物名字和在这里捐钱的人，他们要么是前橄榄球运动员，要么是以某种方式多年来与乔联系在一起的人。所以我的意思是，如果你没有成功的橄榄球队，如果你没有产生收入和胜利的球队，你很快就会发现自己面临减少入学，减少贡献。

00:50:15.010 --> 00:50:18.450
<v Ira Glass>You think it's going to affect the school's finances that this has happened?
<v Ira Glass>你认为这件事发生了会影响学校的财务吗？

00:50:18.450 --> 00:50:57.420
<v Frank Fitzpatrick>I think that's the great fear. I mean, the fear is that if Penn State's image, the image they've sold to these donors and contributors all the years is this squeaky-clean image. And if that's tarnished, that's a lot of potential donations. I was just going back on some of my research I did when I was doing the series of stories about Penn State, and there were quite a few donors who at that time, said, when they had no inkling that anything like this was in the wind, said I'm more than happy to give my money to Penn State, because they do things the right way. If that ever changes, I'll have to make an assessment at that time.
<v Frank Fitzpatrick>我想那是巨大的恐惧。我的意思是，恐惧是如果宾夕法尼亚州立大学的形象，他们多年来卖给这些捐赠者和贡献者的形象是这种纯净无瑕的形象。如果那被玷污了，那就是很多潜在的捐赠。我刚回看了一些我在做关于宾夕法尼亚州立大学故事系列时所做的研究，当时有不少捐赠者说，当他们没有迹象表明有任何像这样的事在酝酿中时，说我非常乐意给宾夕法尼亚州立大学我的钱，因为他们做事的方式正确。如果那改变了，我届时必须做一个评估。

00:50:57.420 --> 00:51:02.370
<v Ira Glass>Wait, wait. They said if that ever changes, I think I'll have to look at it? People actually went that far?
<v Ira Glass>等等，等等。他们说如果那改变了，我想我得看看它？人们竟然走那么远？

00:51:02.370 --> 00:51:24.810
<v Frank Fitzpatrick>Yeah. I mean, because at that time, and for quite a while, people were wondering what this place is going to look like when Joe leaves. You know, Joe's sort of been the guy that's propped up this image. So you can imagine that that assessment is being made by hundreds of different people, even as we speak. Do I continue to give my money to Penn State, or do I want my name associated with something so sordid as this?
<v Frank Fitzpatrick>是的。我的意思是，因为在那个时候，并且有相当长一段时间，人们都在想当乔离开时这个地方会是什么样子。你知道，乔一直是支撑这个形象的人。所以你可以想象，即使我们说话时，数百个不同的人也在做那个评估。我是否继续给宾夕法尼亚州立大学我的钱，还是我想让我的名字与如此肮脏的东西联系在一起？

00:51:24.810 --> 00:51:27.900
<v Ira Glass>Frank Fitzpatrick of the Philadelphia Inquirer.
<v Ira Glass>《费城问询报》的弗兰克·菲茨帕特里克。

00:51:27.900 --> 00:51:55.780
<v Ira Glass>Here is one more thing about how the fate of the football team affects everything in this town. This is another clip from our 2009 show. Nancy Updike, for that show, did this story about the local businesses at Penn State, in the town of State College. The week that we were there, nearly every business owner told us that their businesses were significantly down because of the football team. Because of Penn State's loss to Ohio State the week before. Anyway, this is from her story.
<v Ira Glass>这是关于橄榄球队的命运如何影响这个镇上一切的另一件事。这是我们2009年节目的另一个片段。南希·厄普代克（Nancy Updike）为那个节目做了这个关于宾夕法尼亚州立大学当地企业的故事，在州立学院镇。我们在那里的那个星期，几乎每个企业主都告诉我们，由于橄榄球队，他们的生意大大下降。因为宾夕法尼亚州立大学上一周输给了俄亥俄州立大学。无论如何，这是她故事里的。

00:51:55.780 --> 00:52:20.630
<v Nancy Updike>This is McLanahan's, a sprawling Hearst Castle of a store. It's your basic drugstore, deli produce market, hardware, underwear, beer pong, stationary, grocery, Penn State merchandise emporium, which over the years has acquired and absorbed the stores that used to be next to it, to the point where it's bought up everything it can, and the only way left to expand is to cram more stuff everywhere. And there's always something clamoring for more space.
<v Nancy Updike>这是McLanahan's，一家赫斯特城堡般的庞大商店。它是你的基本药店、熟食农产品市场、硬件、内衣、啤酒乒乓、文具、杂货、宾夕法尼亚州立大学商品商场，多年来它收购并吸收了它旁边的商店，到了它买下一切它能买的东西，唯一扩展的方法是往任何地方塞更多东西。而且总有什么东西在吵着要更多空间。

00:52:20.630 --> 00:52:30.880
<v Nancy Updike>McLanahan's owner Ray Agostinelli says that over and over again, he's had to stock way up on some usually sleepy object that has suddenly become a crazy hot seller.
<v Nancy Updike>McLanahan's老板雷·阿戈斯蒂内利（Ray Agostinelli）说，一遍又一遍，他不得不囤积某种通常冷门的商品，而这些商品突然成了疯狂的热门商品。

00:52:30.880 --> 00:52:41.430
<v Ray Agostinelli>Well, it happened one time with marshmallows, and all of a sudden, all the marshmallows were gone. And here, what it was, was that they'd take them to the football games, and they were throwing marshmallows.
<v Ray Agostinelli>好吧，有一次发生在棉花糖上，突然间，所有的棉花糖都没了。而这里，是他们带它们去橄榄球比赛，他们在扔棉花糖。

00:52:41.430 --> 00:52:43.440
<v Nancy Updike>You said people were throwing marshmallows onto the field?
<v Nancy Updike>你说人们在往球场扔棉花糖？

00:52:43.440 --> 00:52:58.910
<v Ray Agostinelli>Oh yeah, if they did-- They were? Getting pretty dangerous with them. This wasn't too good. And they got outlawed. But the kids would come in and buy all the marshmallows, and we said, wait a minute. We never sold that many marshmallows. What? But it's just whatever the crazes are, you have to keep up with and be there.
<v Ray Agostinelli>噢是的，如果他们做了……他们是在吗？用它们变得相当危险。这不太好。然后它们被取缔了。但孩子们会进来买光所有的棉花糖，我们说，等一下。我们从没卖出过那么多棉花糖。什么？但这只是无论狂热是什么，你都必须跟上并待在那里。

00:52:58.910 --> 00:53:18.030
<v Nancy Updike>And then there are the crazes that never go away. Sex. One of McLanahan's many frank and nonjudgmental offerings is its display, right up front, that is condoms on top, lubricant in the middle, pregnancy tests on the bottom.
<v Nancy Updike>然后有永不消失的狂热。性。McLanahan's的许多坦率和非评判性的产品之一是它的展示，就在最前面，那是上面的避孕套，中间的润滑剂，底部的验孕棒。

00:53:18.030 --> 00:53:32.050
<v Nancy Updike>Ray takes me into the two Penn State merchandise sections of the store. It's a full sensory experience. That's a CD of the school's band in the background. There are seven different kinds of car air fresheners with the Penn State logo, pacifiers, a steering wheel cover.
<v Nancy Updike>雷带我进了商店的两个宾夕法尼亚州立大学商品区。这是一种完整的感官体验。背景中那是学校乐队的CD。有七种不同种类的车用空气清新剂带有宾夕法尼亚州立大学标志，安抚奶嘴，方向盘套。

00:53:32.050 --> 00:53:42.310
<v Nancy Updike>Ray, are you ever amazed at something you never knew that Penn State logo could be put on it? I just saw a remote control, a TV remote--
<v Nancy Updike>雷，你有没有对某种你从未知道宾夕法尼亚州立大学标志可以放在上面的东西感到惊讶？我刚刚看到一个遥控器，电视遥控器——

00:53:42.310 --> 00:53:46.060
<v Ray Agostinelli>You put it on here and it'll sell. You put it on your microphone, it'll sell.
<v Ray Agostinelli>你把它放在这里，它就会卖。你把它放在你的麦克风上，它就会卖。

00:53:46.060 --> 00:54:01.860
<v Nancy Updike>In the almost 50 years Ray has been working at McLanahan's-- he bought it from Bob McLanahan 40 years ago-- Ray has seen the Penn State merchandise in his store go from almost an afterthought, maybe one rack of sweatshirts, to being a third of his business. The only downside is--
<v Nancy Updike>在雷在McLanahan's工作近50年里——他40年前从鲍勃·麦克拉纳汉（Bob McLanahan）那里买下了它——雷看到他店里的宾夕法尼亚州立大学商品从几乎是一个事后想法，也许是一架连帽衫，变成了他生意的一三分之一。唯一负面的是——

00:54:01.860 --> 00:54:07.840
<v Ray Agostinelli>If Penn State would have a losing season, sales would drop dramatically.
<v Ray Agostinelli>如果宾夕法尼亚州立大学有一个输球的赛季，销售额会急剧下降。

00:54:07.840 --> 00:54:08.490
<v Nancy Updike>Oh, is that true?
<v Nancy Updike>哦，那是真的吗？

00:54:08.490 --> 00:54:11.960
<v Ray Agostinelli>Oh my gosh. You can't imagine.
<v Ray Agostinelli>噢天哪。你无法想象。

00:54:11.960 --> 00:54:24.230
<v Nancy Updike>Ray says if Penn State loses one football game, sales of Penn State merch at McLanahan's go down 20%. Not for a day, for a week, and sometimes even longer, until the next winning game.
<v Nancy Updike>雷说如果宾夕法尼亚州立大学输掉一场橄榄球比赛，McLanahan's的宾夕法尼亚州立大学商品销售额会下降20%。不是一天，是一周，有时甚至更长，直到下一场获胜的比赛。

00:54:24.230 --> 00:54:28.910
<v Ira Glass>OK. There's more to that story, but you'll have to go online. That was Nancy Updike.
<v Ira Glass>好的。那个故事还有更多，但你必须上网。那是南希·厄普代克。

00:54:28.910 --> 00:54:49.730
<v Ira Glass>Let's end this hour at a game. Right? A football game. I went to a game when we were there in 2009 with two juniors, Megan Koch and Zach Fliegel. Megan's Nittany Lion memories include the night of her high school homecoming, which also happen to be the night of the 2005 Michigan game. She was all dressed up. Her dad was on the cell phone.
<v Ira Glass>让我们在比赛中结束这一小时。对吧？橄榄球比赛。我们2009年在那儿的时候，我和两个大三学生，梅根·科赫（Megan Koch）和扎克·弗利格尔（Zach Fliegel）去看了比赛。梅根的尼塔尼雄狮（Nittany Lion）回忆包括她高中返校节的那个晚上，那天晚上正好也是2005年密歇根比赛的晚上。她打扮得很漂亮。她爸爸在手机上。

00:54:49.730 --> 00:55:30.750
<v Megan Koch>He was calling me, telling me, play by play, what was going on. So I remember I go inside, and my field hockey team always gets together in like a hallway. And all the parents come around, everyone comes around and we get pictures taken. And I remember my dad called me, and he's like, you're not going to believe this, but Michigan just scored and we lost with two seconds left on the clock. And I remember I just hung up the phone, and I was like, oh my gosh. I started like crying. And one of the hockey moms came up to me, and she was like, Megan, what's wrong, you know? And I was like, Penn State just lost. And she was-- she laughed. And I have never been so close to punching a parent.
<v Megan Koch>他给我打电话，告诉我，一球一球地，正在发生什么。所以我记得我进去，我的曲棍球队总是在像走廊里的地方聚在一起。所有的父母都过来了，每个人都过来了，我们拍了照片。我记得我爸爸给我打电话，他就像，你不会相信这个，但密歇根刚得分，我们在时钟还剩两秒时输了。我记得我只是挂了电话，就像，噢天哪。我开始像是哭了起来。其中一个曲棍球妈妈向我走过来，她就像，梅根，你怎么了，你知道吗？我说，宾夕法尼亚州立大学刚输了。她就是……她笑了。我从来没有那么接近去打一个家长。

00:55:30.750 --> 00:55:38.970
<v Ira Glass>Zach also has childhood memories of Penn State games. On our way to the stadium, we pass some little boys in the tailgate, tossing around a football.
<v Ira Glass>扎克也有关于宾夕法尼亚州立大学比赛的童年记忆。在去体育场的路上，我们经过一些尾门派对里的小男孩，正在投掷橄榄球。

00:55:38.970 --> 00:55:43.540
<v Zach Fliegel>I used to be that kid, throwing the football around in between cars and stuff.
<v Zach Fliegel>我过去就是那个孩子，在车子中间之类的扔橄榄球。

00:55:43.540 --> 00:55:53.740
<v Ira Glass>The Lions played, the Lions won. It was the last home game of the year, and seniors down in the first row were crying. Zach said even a bad game, being in the stadium, in the student section, was worth it.
<v Ira Glass>雄狮队打比赛，雄狮队赢了。那是那一年的最后一场主场比赛，坐在第一排的老年人都在哭。扎克说即使是一场糟糕的比赛，在体育场里，在学生区，也是值得的。

00:55:53.740 --> 00:55:59.800
<v Zach Fliegel>I live for this. I'll probably be coming here until I'm old and wrinkled.
<v Zach Fliegel>我为这个而活。我可能直到我老得满脸皱纹都会来这里。

00:55:59.800 --> 00:56:03.910
<v Ira Glass>Really? So like, 50 years from now, where will you be sitting? Point to the spot.
<v Ira Glass>真的吗？所以50年后，你会坐在哪里？指指那个点。

00:56:03.910 --> 00:56:10.340
<v Zach Fliegel>Right over there. Hopefully in WC, WD on the 30, 40 yard line, maybe.
<v Zach Fliegel>就在那边。希望在WC，WD在30，40码线上，可能。

00:56:10.340 --> 00:56:12.030
<v Ira Glass>Show me the seats where you want?
<v Ira Glass>给我看你想坐的座位？

00:56:12.030 --> 00:56:25.220
<v Ira Glass>And he pointed a third of the way up, 30 yard line. I went around to his friends in the student section. Nobody hesitated. 50 yard line, skybox, 30 yard line. They all wanted to come back.
<v Ira Glass>他指了三分之一处，30码线。我转到了学生区他的朋友那里。没有人犹豫。50码线，豪华包厢，30码线。他们都想回来。

00:56:25.220 --> 00:56:34.550
<v Ira Glass>Then when I emailed Zach this week, and asked if he still thought he'd be back in the year 2059, he said it depends on how the school handles the charges in the next few months.
<v Ira Glass>当我本周给扎克发邮件，问他是否还认为他会在2059年回来时，他说这取决于学校在未来几个月如何处理这些指控。

00:56:34.550 --> 00:56:46.840
<v Ira Glass>["WE ARE PENN STATE" CHANT]
<v Ira Glass>[高呼“我们是宾夕法尼亚州立大学”]

00:56:46.840 --> 00:57:01.220
<v Ira Glass>Well, our program today was produced by Sarah Koenig, Jonathan Menjivar, Miki Meek and me, with Alex Blumberg, Ben Calhoun, Jane Feltes, Lisa Pollak, Robyn Semien, Aaron Scott, Alissa Shipp, Brian Reed, and Nancy Updike. Our senior producer is Julie Snyder, Seth Lind is our production manager, Emily Condon is our office manager.
<v Ira Glass>好吧，我们今天的节目是由莎拉·柯尼格，乔纳森·门吉瓦尔（Jonathan Menjivar），米基·米克（Miki Meek）和我制作，还有亚历克斯·布隆伯格（Alex Blumberg），本·卡尔霍恩（Ben Calhoun），简·费尔特斯（Jane Feltes），丽莎·波拉克（Lisa Pollak），罗宾·塞米恩（Robyn Semien），亚伦·斯科特（Aaron Scott），阿丽莎·希普（Alissa Shipp），布莱恩·里德（Brian Reed）和南希·厄普代克（Nancy Updike）。我们的高级制片人是朱莉·斯奈德（Julie Snyder），塞思·林德（Seth Lind）是我们的制作经理，艾米莉·康登（Emily Condon）是我们的办公室经理。

00:57:01.220 --> 00:57:03.920
<v Ira Glass>[ACKNOWLEDGMENTS]
<v Ira Glass>[致谢]

00:57:03.920 --> 00:57:19.530
<v Ira Glass>Our website, where you can hear our original 2009 show about Penn State, "Number One Party School," for absolutely free, or you can hear any other show we have ever made, our download our apps, or do all kinds of stuff, thisamericanlife.org. This American Life is distributed by Public Radio International.
<v Ira Glass>我们的网站，在那里你可以免费听到我们2009年关于宾夕法尼亚州立大学的原始节目，“第一派对学校”，或者你可以听到我们制作过的任何其他节目，下载我们的应用，或者做各种各样的事情，thisamericanlife.org。《This American Life》由公共广播国际公司发行。

00:57:19.530 --> 00:58:02.310
<v Ira Glass>[FUNDING CREDITS]
<v Ira Glass>[资金来源致谢]

00:58:02.310 --> 00:58:12.750
<v Ira Glass>WBEZ management oversight for our show by our boss, Mr. Torey Malatia, who kept holding his personnel conferences at a big boy restaurant near the highway-- I know!-- until--
<v Ira Glass>WBEZ管理对我们节目的监督由我们的老板，托雷·马拉蒂亚（Torey Malatia）先生，他一直坚持在高速公路附近的一家大男孩餐厅举行他的人事会议——我知道！——直到——

00:58:12.750 --> 00:58:20.070
<v Eileen>They had a big boy meeting, and they sat him down, they had a sit-down, and they said-- you're not going to do this again, right?
<v Eileen>他们开了一个大男孩会议，让他坐下来，进行了一次谈话，说……你不能再这样做了，对吧？

00:58:20.070 --> 00:58:49.110
<v Ira Glass>I'm Ira Glass. Back next week with more stories of This American Life.
<v Ira Glass>我是艾拉·格拉斯。下周带着《This American Life》的更多故事回来。

00:58:49.110 --> 00:58:54.340
<v Announcer>PRI. Public Radio International.
<v Announcer>PRI。公共广播国际公司。

00:58:54.340 --> 00:58:59.340
<v Ira Glass>Next week in the podcast of This American Life. There's this children's book, a real one, called Nobody's Family is Going to Change. That is an oddly menacing title, if you ask me. Nobody's Family is Going to Change. It is a thought that occurs to you when you go home for Thanksgiving or Christmas, for sure. We try to figure out whether it's true. Next week on the podcast or on your local Public Radio station.
<v Ira Glass>下周在《This American Life》的播客中。有这本儿童书，一本真正的书，叫《没人家的家庭会改变》（Nobody's Family is Going to Change）。如果你问我，那是一个奇怪的威胁性标题。《没人家的家庭会改变》。当你回家过感恩节或圣诞节时，这确实是一个出现在你脑海中的想法。我们尝试弄清楚它是否是真的。下周在播客上或在你的当地公共广播电台。
